// Deep per-task detail for the marketing plan, keyed by `${dayDate}#${sortOrder}`.
// Blocks (heading + body): strategy/creative/copy for posts, why/how for setup, ad
// structure for ads, what/copy for emails. Authored by the marketing-deep-detail
// workflow, then rebranded to Abi Peters Skin Clinic with the two-track nurture.
export type DeepBlock = { h: string; b: string };
export const DEEP: Record<string, DeepBlock[]> = {"2026-08-30#1":[{"h":"Why","b":"Both Google Business Profiles are expired, which means when a Winchester or Havant woman searches 'skin clinic near me' or 'Abi Peters', you either do not appear or appear as unclaimed. That is the single highest-intent free traffic there is: someone actively looking for a clinic today. A live, verified profile also unlocks the review requests and the map pin that convince a stranger you are real, local and trusted. Fix this before spending a penny on ads."},{"h":"How","b":"1. Go to business.google.com and sign in with the clinic Google account (the one tied to accounts@abipetersaesthetics.co.uk). 2. You should see both listings flagged as 'expired' or needing action; click the Bedhampton one first. 3. Hit 'Get verified' or 'Reclaim this profile' and choose a verification method: video is fastest, so record the short clip it asks for showing the premises and signage. 4. While you wait for verification, update the essentials: correct opening hours, phone, website URL, the 'nurse-led skin clinic' category, and 3 to 5 recent photos of the clinic and Abi. 5. Repeat the whole process for the second profile. 6. Set a reminder to check back in 3 to 5 days: Google emails when each is verified, and only then will edits and reviews go live."}],"2026-08-30#2":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"This is the seed that makes the whole content engine cheap and fast: capture 6 Reel ideas now so the batch-film session on 2 Sep has a script and you never sit staring at the camera wondering what to say. Each idea should map to the funnel: some pull cold Winchester strangers in (education and myth-busting), some warm up the 443 leads (behind the scenes, meet Abi), and one or two drive the actual Bedhampton offer. Note them in your phone the moment they come, because the best ones arrive between clients, not on demand."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Open your Notes app and title it 'Reel ideas Sep'. Aim for a mix across these proven angles so you are not filming six of the same thing: (1) The one thing I do before any treatment (the skin analysis, education). (2) Three skincare myths I hear every week as a nurse (myth-bust). (3) What a skin analysis actually looks like (film over your own shoulder at the clinic). (4) Why I trained as a nurse first, then went into skin (trust and story). (5) The Winchester clinic coming soon, a peek at Jewry Street (local hype, warms the leads). (6) A skincare ingredient people waste money on (value, saveable). For each, jot the hook line in the note, not just the topic."},{"h":"Copy","b":"In the note, write each idea as a one-line hook you would actually say to camera, because the hook is 80 percent of the Reel. For example: 'Before any treatment I do this one thing and it changes everything.' 'You are probably wasting money on this ingredient.' 'I am a nurse first. Here is why that matters for your face.' 'Everyone asks what a skin analysis actually is, so here it is.' Keep the note open all week and add or swap lines as better ones land. Bring this exact list to the film session on 2 Sep."}],"2026-08-31#11":[{"h":"What to check","b":"You are switching the two Bedhampton ad sets (Cold prospecting and Warm retargeting) from paused to active in Meta Ads Manager. Before you flip them on, confirm four things are correct so you are not spending blind: the daily budget on each set, the audience geography (Cold should ring Bedhampton and Havant, Warm should be website visitors and engagers), the start with no end date, and that the ad links to the Bedhampton free-analysis booking page not a dead URL. Confirm the Meta pixel is firing, because it has been dead since December 2025 and without it you cannot see cost per booked analysis, only cost per click."},{"h":"What good looks like","b":"Your pre-launch Winchester leads came in at about 3.08 pounds each, so treat that as your rough cost-per-lead ceiling for the Cold set; anything under 4 pounds in the first few days is healthy for a warm-up. The number that actually matters is cost per booked skin analysis, not cost per click or per lead: at a premium clinic a booked analysis in the 15 to 30 pound range is very good, because that person is sitting in your chair. Give it 48 to 72 hours before judging, let Meta exit the learning phase, and do not touch the budget in the first two days. If the Warm set (retargeting) is not clearly cheaper per result than the Cold set within a week, the audience or offer needs a look."}],"2026-08-31#12":[{"h":"Why","b":"The Winchester clinic opens on 2 November, and a Google Business Profile takes days to verify and weeks to build the trust signals (reviews, photos, Q and A) that win the map pack. Creating it now, ahead of opening, means that by launch you already rank when someone searches 'skin clinic Winchester' or 'aesthetics Jewry Street'. It also gives the 443 warm leads somewhere real to land and check you out, and it is completely free traffic you would otherwise pay Meta for."},{"h":"How","b":"1. At business.google.com, click 'Add business' then 'Add a single business'. 2. Name it exactly as the brand reads elsewhere (Abi Peters Skin Clinic) and pick the primary category 'Skin care clinic' or 'Aesthetician', with secondary categories like 'Facial spa'. 3. Enter the address 9A Jewry Street, Winchester, and its postcode. 4. Add phone, the website URL, and set opening hours (if not open yet, use the 'opening date' field so Google shows 'Opens 2 Nov'). 5. Choose video verification and film the short walkthrough it requests; if the unit is mid-fit-out, postcard verification is the fallback, so request it now to allow postal time. 6. Once submitted, add 5 or more photos (exterior, signage, treatment room, Abi) and write the 'from the business' description mentioning nurse-led, Winchester and natural results. Do not name any prescription treatment in the description."}],"2026-09-01#21":[{"h":"What","b":"You need TWO separate follow-up sequences in GHL, because a Bedhampton local and a Winchester lead are on completely different journeys. A Bedhampton contact can walk in now; a Winchester contact is waiting for a clinic that opens on 2 November. Tag every contact as Bedhampton or Winchester (the 443 pre-launch leads are Winchester), and send each the matching track below. Both re-warm with value and honesty, then convert: Bedhampton to a booked analysis now, Winchester to a Founding Client spot for November, with an optional early trip to Bedhampton."},{"h":"Copy, Bedhampton follow-up","b":"BEDHAMPTON TRACK (local leads + existing Bedhampton clients: they can come in now)\n\nSMS 1 (day 0): Hi {name}, it's Abi from Abi Peters Skin Clinic. I'd love to see you at my Bedhampton clinic for a complimentary skin analysis, plus 15% off any treatment you book after. It's on until 31 Oct. Want me to find you a time? Reply YES x\n\nEmail 1 (day 0) Subject: Your complimentary skin analysis, {name}. Body: Hi {name}, thanks for your interest in Abi Peters Skin Clinic. I'd love to invite you to my Bedhampton clinic for a complimentary skin analysis: a relaxed 20 minutes where I look properly at your skin and give you an honest plan, no pressure. Book a treatment after and it's 15% off. It's only on until 31 October. [ Book my complimentary analysis ] See you soon, Abi\n\nSMS 2 (day 3, no reply): Hi {name}, Abi here, just checking you saw this. Your complimentary skin analysis and 15% off is still open at my Bedhampton clinic (until 31 Oct). Shall I book you in? Reply YES x\n\nEmail 2 (day 6) Subject: Why I start every client with a skin analysis. Body: Hi {name}, most skin problems aren't what people think. As a nurse I want the honest answer, not the biggest sale, so I never recommend a treatment before I've properly looked at your skin. That's all a complimentary analysis is: 20 minutes, a real look, a plan that fits you. And 15% off if you book after. On until 31 October at my Bedhampton clinic. [ Book ] Abi x\n\nSMS 3 (day 12, last nudge): Last nudge {name}, the complimentary skin analysis and 15% off ends 31 Oct and my Bedhampton diary is filling up. Grab a slot: {link} x"},{"h":"Copy, Winchester follow-up","b":"WINCHESTER TRACK (the 443 founding leads: their clinic opens 2 Nov)\n\nSMS 1 (day 0): Hi {name}, it's Abi from Abi Peters Skin Clinic. Big news: your Winchester clinic opens on 2 November! I'd love to save you a Founding Client spot (priority booking plus perks). And if you can't wait, you're welcome at my Bedhampton clinic now for a complimentary skin analysis and 15% off. Which sounds good? Just reply x\n\nEmail 1 (day 0) Subject: Winchester news, {name} (and a spot with your name on it). Body: Hi {name}, a little while ago you registered your interest in skin care in Winchester, and I have news: I'm opening Abi Peters Skin Clinic on Jewry Street this November. You're on my founding list, which means first pick of the opening diary, a complimentary add-on with your first treatment, and founding pricing locked in for good. Want me to hold you a Founding spot? [ Reserve my Founding spot ] Can't wait until November? Pop to my Bedhampton clinic now for a complimentary skin analysis, 15% off after, well worth the short drive. Abi\n\nEmail 2 (day 6) Subject: Why I'm a nurse before anything else. Body: Hi {name}, I trained as a registered nurse before I ever touched skin, and it shapes everything about the Winchester clinic: safety first, honesty always, natural results over overdone. That is what your Founding spot is a part of. Shall I reserve yours? [ Reserve my Founding spot ] Abi x\n\nSMS 3 (October): {name}, Founding Client spots for Winchester are filling up and we open 2 Nov. Want me to hold yours? Priority booking and founding pricing locked in. Reply YES x"}],"2026-09-01#22":[{"h":"Why","b":"This is the operational switch that makes the Bedhampton September to October promotion real: the complimentary skin analysis plus 15 percent off any treatment booked after it, running until 31 October. If the offer is not actually live in the booking system with a clear way to redeem it, every ad, post and email you send drives people to a dead end and you burn goodwill and ad spend. Turning it on both to staff and to the system means anyone who walks in, calls or books online gets exactly what the marketing promised, with no awkward confusion at the desk."},{"h":"How","b":"1. In GHL, create a bookable service or calendar called 'Complimentary Skin Analysis (Bedhampton)' set to 20 minutes, free, and location-locked to Bedhampton only so nobody books it against Winchester. 2. Create a discount code or manual note for '15 percent off first treatment booked after analysis, valid to 31 Oct' and add it as an internal reminder on the analysis appointment type. 3. On the website and booking page, make sure the offer wording matches exactly: complimentary analysis, then 15 percent off anything booked after, ends 31 October. 4. Abi: brief anyone at the desk or on the phone so they can explain and apply the 15 percent without hesitation, and know the offer is Bedhampton only and ends 31 Oct. 5. Do a test booking yourself end to end to confirm it works before any ad traffic hits it. 6. Set the calendar to auto-close bookings for this service after 31 October."}],"2026-09-02#31":[{"h":"What to film","b":"Film all six in one sitting using the ideas noted on 30 Aug, so you have two weeks of Reels ready. Shot list: (1) 'The one thing I do before any treatment' talking to camera about the skin analysis, 20 to 25 seconds, then a 10 second cutaway of you actually looking at skin under the clinic light. (2) 'Three skincare myths I hear every week', count them on your fingers, 30 seconds. (3) A silent over-the-shoulder clip of a real skin analysis in progress (get consent, no client face needed), for B-roll and its own Reel. (4) 'Why I am a nurse first', the trust and story piece, 25 seconds, warm and unscripted. (5) 'Winchester, we are coming', hold your phone outside or near a photo of 9A Jewry Street and tease the November opening, 20 seconds. (6) 'The ingredient you are wasting money on', one clear tip, 20 seconds. Also grab 4 or 5 loose B-roll clips: hands, product textures, the clinic space, the sign, you laughing between takes. Do not name any prescription treatment in any clip meant for paid promotion."},{"h":"How","b":"Film facing a window so natural light falls on your face, never with the window behind you. Phone at eye level, propped on a stack of books or a small tripod, in vertical 9:16. Keep each clip 15 to 30 seconds and film every one two or three times so you have options; the second take is always better than the first. Say the hook in the first two seconds before you settle in. Film more than you think you need, especially B-roll, because loose clips are gold when you edit. Wipe the lens, check your framing once, then just talk to the camera like it is a client you like."}],"2026-09-02#32":[{"h":"Why","b":"Reviews are the currency that turns a stranger into a booking, and the Google map pack ranks clinics partly on review volume and freshness. Right now every happy Bedhampton client walks out and the goodwill evaporates because nobody asks them to leave a word. Switching on automatic review requests means every completed appointment quietly triggers a friendly ask, building a steady stream of five-star reviews that will also power the new Winchester profile's credibility from day one. It is the cheapest trust you will ever build."},{"h":"How","b":"1. In GHL, go to Reputation or Reviews and connect the verified Bedhampton Google Business Profile (this only works once the profile from 30 Aug is verified). 2. Create a review-request workflow triggered when an appointment status is marked 'completed' or 'showed'. 3. Add a short wait step of about 2 hours so the ask arrives after they have left, not while they are still in the chair. 4. Set it to send by SMS first (highest open rate) with an email fallback; keep the copy warm: 'Hi {name}, it was lovely seeing you today. If you have 20 seconds, a quick Google review really helps a small clinic like ours: {link}. Thank you, Abi.' 5. Insert the direct Google review link so it opens straight to the star box. 6. Turn the workflow on, then test it against your own completed test appointment to confirm the message fires and the link lands on the right profile."}],"2026-09-03#41":[{"h":"Objective","b":"Build a Google Search Ads campaign now but leave it paused, ready to catch high-intent local searches when you choose to launch. Search ads are bottom-of-funnel: unlike Meta, these people are actively typing 'skin clinic Winchester' or 'skin analysis Havant' right now, so they convert at a higher rate and deserve to be ready. Objective is Leads (booked skin analyses and consultations), optimising for calls and booking-page conversions, not raw clicks."},{"h":"Audience","b":"Two campaigns split by location: one radius around Bedhampton and Havant (about 5 to 7 miles) for the live September offer, and one around Winchester (about 5 miles, centred on Jewry Street) for the launch. Layer in-market audiences for 'beauty and wellness' and 'skin care' as observation. No need to over-target age or gender on Search since the keywords already qualify intent, but you can nudge bids toward women 30 to 55 once data comes in. Set language to English and location to 'people in or regularly in' your target area, not 'interested in', so you do not pay for tourists."},{"h":"Budget","b":"Start each campaign at about 10 to 15 pounds a day, so roughly 20 to 30 pounds daily total once both run. Aesthetics keywords can be pricey per click, so cap it and watch cost per booked analysis rather than clicks. Use a manual or maximise-conversions bid strategy only after the conversion tracking is confirmed firing; until then, keep it paused as instructed."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Responsive Search Ads with tight ad groups: one group for 'skin analysis' terms, one for 'skin clinic near me' terms, one for 'aesthetics Winchester'. Headlines to rotate: 'Nurse-Led Skin Clinic', 'Complimentary Skin Analysis', 'Havant and Winchester', 'Natural Results, Honest Advice', 'Book Your Skin Consultation'. Descriptions: 'A registered nurse looks at your skin under proper light and tells you what it actually needs.' and 'Bedhampton clinic, Winchester opening November. Book your consultation today.' Add sitelink, call and location extensions. Do not name any prescription or POM treatment as a keyword or in copy, and avoid any 'guaranteed results' language."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Primary headline set: 'Nurse-Led Skin Clinic | Bedhampton and Winchester | Book a Skin Analysis'. Description: 'Not sure what your skin needs? A registered nurse looks properly and gives you honest advice, no hard sell. Complimentary analysis at Bedhampton. Book today.' Keep everything paused after building. Before you ever launch, confirm conversion tracking (booking confirmation page or GHL call event) is set up in Google Ads, because without it you are flying blind on cost per booked analysis, which is the only number that matters."}],"2026-09-04#51":[{"h":"What","b":"Send the September offer to your entire email list (existing Bedhampton clients and past enquiries, separate from the 443 Winchester nurture leads who are on their own re-warm track). The goal is to fill the free skin analysis diary at Bedhampton while the offer runs, and remind lapsed clients you are there. This is a single broadcast, not an automation: warm, valuable, one clear call to action to book the complimentary analysis and claim the 15 percent off booked after."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Subject: The 20 minutes that changes how you spend on your skin. Preview text: Complimentary, and only until 31 October. \n\nBody: Hi {name}, \n\nIt is Abi here. Before I ever recommend a single treatment, I do one thing, and honestly it is the most useful 20 minutes in skincare: a proper skin analysis. \n\nI sit you under good light, actually look at your skin, and tell you what is really going on. Not what an advert wants to sell you, what your skin actually needs. Sometimes that means a treatment. Sometimes it means saving your money and changing one thing at home. Either way you leave knowing. \n\nUntil 31 October at my Bedhampton clinic this analysis is complimentary, and if you book any treatment after it you get 15 percent off. No pressure to book anything at all. \n\nThere are only so many slots in the diary before the end of October, so if you have been meaning to sort your skin out, now is the moment. \n\nBook your complimentary analysis here: {link} \n\nSee you soon, \nAbi \n\nP.S. This one is Bedhampton only and ends 31 October. Winchester friends, your turn is coming very soon. \n\nSend from the clinic email in GHL, mobile-friendly single column, one button linking to the Bedhampton analysis booking calendar."}],"2026-09-04#52":[{"h":"Why","b":"Loading two weeks of posts into the scheduler in one sitting is what keeps the clinic visible without you touching it daily, which is exactly what falls apart when a business gets busy. Using the six Reels filmed on 2 Sep plus supporting static and carousel posts, you queue a consistent rhythm that feeds the funnel: education to pull cold Winchester strangers, story and behind-the-scenes to warm the 443 leads, and offer posts to drive the Bedhampton bookings. A planned fortnight also lets you balance the mix so you are not all-sell, which is what kills a premium, trust-led brand."},{"h":"How","b":"1. In GHL's social planner (or Meta Business Suite if you prefer), connect the Facebook and Instagram accounts, which are already linked. 2. Plan roughly 3 to 4 posts a week across the fortnight, so about 7 or 8 slots: aim for a rough ratio of 3 educational or myth-bust, 2 story or behind-the-scenes, 2 offer, 1 Winchester teaser. 3. Drop each of the six filmed Reels into its own slot as the backbone, then fill the gaps with a carousel (for example 'Three myths' as swipeable static cards) and a simple offer graphic for the free analysis. 4. Write each caption in Abi's voice with a scroll-stopping first line, one clear call to action, and 4 to 6 local hashtags such as #WinchesterAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #NurseLedAesthetics #HavantSkinClinic #SkinHealth. 5. Space Reels out so two do not land back to back, and post at sensible times (early morning, lunch, or 8pm). 6. Schedule the whole fortnight, then set one reminder to check comments and reply, because engagement in the first hour is what makes a Reel travel."}],"2026-09-06#101":[{"h":"Why","b":"This is your weekly steering moment: five minutes that stop the whole plan drifting. Phase 1 runs on scheduled posts, a live Google campaign and a warm Winchester list, so one unnoticed gap (a post that did not publish, a booking link that 404s) quietly costs you real analysis bookings. A quick Monday check keeps momentum and means you never find out on a Friday that nothing went out."},{"h":"How","b":"1) Open the content scheduler (GHL Social Planner or Meta Business Suite Planner) and confirm this week's posts show a green 'Scheduled' status with the right dates and images attached. 2) Click into POST 1's link in the caption and load it yourself: does the booking or comment call to action actually work on mobile. 3) In GHL, check the 'skin analysis' calendar has open slots for the next 14 days. 4) Glance at Meta Ads Manager and Google Ads for the word 'Active' on each campaign, not 'Paused' or 'Rejected'. 5) Note anything broken in a single message to Abi so it is fixed before Monday lunch."}],"2026-09-07#111":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"This is the opening authority post of the whole campaign, so it sets the tone: expert, calm, nurse-led, never salesy. Its job is to plant the idea that a skin analysis is the sensible first step before spending on any treatment, which primes everyone for the free-analysis offer post four days later. It also warms the algorithm and the audience with pure value, so the later offer post does not feel like a cold pitch."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Film a 20 to 30 second Reel of Abi in the Bedhampton treatment room, good natural light, talking straight to camera. On-screen hook text for the first frame: 'Before you spend a penny on your skin, do this.' Show a quick two-second cutaway of the analysis in action: the lamp, Abi looking closely at a client's cheek, a skincare shelf. End on Abi's face, warm and smiling. Keep it unpolished and real, not a produced advert."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Most people book a treatment before anyone has actually looked at their skin. I do it the other way round. A skin analysis is 20 minutes under proper light where I read what your skin is really doing: the dryness you think is sensitivity, the sun damage hiding under makeup, the barrier that needs repair before anything else. Then, and only then, do we talk treatments. It is the single most useful thing I do, and right now it is complimentary at my Bedhampton clinic until 31 October, with 15 percent off anything you book afterwards. Comment ANALYSIS or tap the link in my bio. #WinchesterAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #NurseLedAesthetics #SkinHealth #BedhamptonClinic"}],"2026-09-08#121":[{"h":"Why","b":"Google Search catches people at the exact moment they are typing 'skin clinic near me' or 'skin analysis Havant', which is far warmer intent than a social scroll. A soft 10 pounds a day lets you learn what converts without burning budget while you find your feet. Starting now, in Phase 1, means the account has data and a track record before the bigger Winchester push in November."},{"h":"How","b":"1) Go to ads.google.com and sign in with the clinic Google account. 2) Click New Campaign, choose the goal 'Leads', then campaign type 'Search'. 3) Set 'Ways to reach your goal' to Website visits and Phone calls, using the booking page URL and clinic number. 4) Location: set a radius of about 8 miles around Havant and Bedhampton (Target 'presence: people in this location' only, not 'interest'). 5) Budget: type 10 into the daily budget, bidding set to 'Maximise clicks' with a manual max CPC cap of about 1.50 pounds to start. 6) Build one ad group 'Skin Analysis' with keywords in phrase match: \"skin analysis\", \"skin clinic havant\", \"skin consultation hampshire\". 7) Write 3 headlines (Nurse-Led Skin Clinic; Free Skin Analysis; Book Online Today) and 2 descriptions mentioning the complimentary analysis and 15 percent off. 8) Add sitelink and call assets. 9) Set the campaign live and diarise a check for 15 September."}],"2026-09-09#131":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"People buy from a person they trust, and the entire brand rests on Abi being a real registered nurse, not a faceless clinic. This post builds the human bond that makes the clinical authority land: warmth first, credentials second. It sits between the authority post and the offer post so that when the offer arrives, viewers already feel they know and like the person behind it."},{"h":"Creative","b":"A softer, slower Reel or a 4-photo carousel. If Reel: Abi to camera, no hard sell, telling her own story in 25 seconds. Opening on-screen text: 'How a nurse ended up doing skin.' B-roll options: Abi in uniform earlier in her career, hands washing, the Bedhampton sign, a genuine laugh with a client. If carousel: slide 1 a lovely portrait of Abi, slides 2 to 4 short handwritten-feel captions about why she moved into skin health."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Hi, I am Abi. Before I ever did aesthetics, I was a nurse, and honestly I still think like one. That means I look at your skin the way I would assess anything: what is actually going on, what is the safest route, what will genuinely help. No trends, no pressure, no selling you something you do not need. I started this clinic in Bedhampton because I wanted skin care that felt honest and expert at the same time, and soon I get to bring it to Winchester too. If that is the kind of clinic you have been looking for, come and say hello. Link in my bio. #NurseLedAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #WinchesterAesthetics #MeetYourNurse #SkinExpert"}],"2026-09-11#151":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"This is the conversion post the two previous posts were building towards: the clear ask. Authority and human warmth have been established, so now you make the offer explicit and give one frictionless action. Its whole job is booked analyses before the 31 October deadline, so the deadline and the booking link do the heavy lifting. Scarcity is honest here because the free analysis genuinely ends with October."},{"h":"Creative","b":"A bright, confident static or short 15 second Reel. If Reel: Abi holding up her diary or gesturing to the booking screen, on-screen hook 'Free skin analysis, but only until October.' If static: a clean graphic with the offer in the brand palette, a photo of the clinic, and the words 'Complimentary Skin Analysis plus 15 percent off any treatment booked after.' Make the booking link and the word ANALYSIS impossible to miss."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Right, here is the offer, plainly. Until 31 October at my Bedhampton clinic, your skin analysis is completely free. Twenty minutes where I look properly at your skin and tell you the truth about it, then you get 15 percent off anything you decide to book afterwards. No obligation to book a single thing on the day. It is the easiest, lowest-risk way to finally understand your skin and get a plan that actually fits it. Slots are limited and October always fills up fast. Tap the link in my bio to book, or comment ANALYSIS and I will send it over. #HampshireAesthetics #SkinAnalysis #NurseLedAesthetics #BedhamptonClinic #HavantSkin"}],"2026-09-13#201":[{"h":"What to film","b":"Batch-record the six Reels that will carry the next fortnight so nothing is filmed in a panic. 1) Authority, skin boosters explained: Abi to camera describing what a skin booster is in plain words (for the 14 Sep post). 2) Human, behind the scenes: Abi setting up the room, prrepping, why nurse-led matters (16 Sep). 3) Proof, a piece to camera introducing a client review, leaving a gap to overlay the quote (18 Sep). 4) Authority, one skincare myth busted, sharp opening line (21 Sep). 5) Human, a day in the clinic montage narrated by Abi (23 Sep). 6) Proof plus offer, Abi inviting people to book with the testimonial theme (25 Sep). Also grab 60 seconds of loose b-roll: hands, products, the lamp, the sign, the diary."},{"h":"How","b":"Film near a window with the light falling on Abi's face, never behind her. Phone horizontal or vertical but locked at eye level on a small tripod or propped stack of books, never handheld and looking down. Keep each clip 15 to 30 seconds and record two takes of every one so David has a choice. Film a little extra at the start and end of each clip for trimming room, and shoot all six in the same outfit-friendly block so they can be spaced across the fortnight without looking rushed."}],"2026-09-13#202":[{"h":"Why","b":"Scheduling the week in one sitting means the posts actually go out on their planned days even when the clinic gets busy, which is when consistency usually collapses. Consistent posting is what keeps the algorithm showing you to the warm Winchester and Havant audience, and it protects Abi's time so she is not writing captions between clients. Batching also lets you eyeball the week as a whole and catch any two posts that feel too similar."},{"h":"How","b":"1) Open the GHL Social Planner (or Meta Business Suite Planner). 2) Drop in this week's Reels from the batch filmed today, one per planned date. 3) Paste each approved caption, check the hashtags and the booking link resolve. 4) Set each to publish to both Facebook and Instagram at a consistent good-time slot (early evening tends to suit the 30 to 55 audience). 5) Confirm each shows 'Scheduled', then screenshot the week's calendar view and send it to Abi so she knows what is going live and when."}],"2026-09-14#211":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"Skin boosters are a flagship treatment and a strong search term for the clinic, so explaining them in plain English positions Abi as the trustworthy expert on exactly the thing people are quietly googling. This post educates rather than sells, which builds authority and quietly qualifies the audience: people who understand what a booster does are warmer leads. It keeps the free-analysis offer alive by ending on 'a booster is only right if your skin needs it, which is what the analysis is for'."},{"h":"Creative","b":"A 25 to 30 second explainer Reel, Abi to camera in the treatment room. First-frame hook text: 'Skin boosters are not fillers. Here is the difference.' Use simple on-screen labels as she talks (hydration, glow, deep in the skin). Optional cutaway to a product vial or the treatment area, no needles-in-skin close-ups that could feel clinical or breach ad sensibilities. End on Abi's face."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Let me clear up the biggest mix-up I hear. A skin booster is not a filler. Fillers add shape. A skin booster works underneath the surface to hydrate and improve the quality of your skin itself, so over time it looks healthier and more you, not different. Think of it as feeding your skin rather than changing your face. It is brilliant for that tired, dull, dehydrated look that no amount of moisturiser seems to fix. But, and this matters, it is only worth doing if your skin actually needs it, which is exactly what a skin analysis tells us. Book your complimentary analysis at Bedhampton before 31 October, link in bio. #SkinBoosters #HampshireAesthetics #NurseLedAesthetics #SkinHealth #WinchesterAesthetics"}],"2026-09-15#221":[{"h":"What","b":"Send a genuine value newsletter to the warm list (the 443 Winchester leads plus existing Bedhampton contacts) that gives before it asks. The goal is to stay top of mind, prove expertise, and gently drive a few free-analysis bookings before October closes, all without feeling like a sales blast. It also keeps the list warm and deliverable for the bigger Winchester emails to come in the autumn."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Subject: The 20 minutes that save you money on your skin\n\nHi {name},\n\nMost of us buy skincare the wrong way round. We spot a product, or an ad, or a friend's recommendation, and we buy it hoping it is the answer. Sometimes it is. Often it is money spent on the wrong thing for our skin.\n\nHere is what I do instead, and what I would gently suggest for you. Start with a skin analysis. Twenty minutes where I look at your skin under proper light and tell you what is actually going on: whether that redness is sensitivity or something else, whether you need hydration or repair, what is worth your money and what is not.\n\nUntil 31 October, that analysis is completely free at my Bedhampton clinic, and you get 15 percent off anything you choose to book afterwards. No pressure to book anything on the day. Just an honest, expert look at your skin and a plan that fits it.\n\nIf you have been meaning to sort your skin out properly, this is the easy first step.\n\nBook here: {link}\n\nWarmly,\nAbi\nRegistered Nurse and founder, Abi Peters Skin Clinic"}],"2026-09-16#231":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"Midweek humanising post that deepens trust by pulling back the curtain on how the clinic actually runs. Where the offer posts ask, this one gives: it shows the care, hygiene and thought behind nurse-led practice, which is the exact reassurance a 30 to 55 woman wants before letting someone near her face. It reinforces the clinical-safety differentiator that separates Abi from beauticians offering the same treatments."},{"h":"Creative","b":"A behind-the-scenes Reel, 20 to 30 seconds, filmed as Abi preps the room. Hook text frame one: 'What nurse-led actually means.' B-roll: hands being cleaned, a sterile tray being laid out, products lined up, the consent form, Abi checking notes. Warm, calm pace, soft background music. Voiceover from Abi over the top rather than straight-to-camera, so it feels like a genuine glimpse."},{"h":"Copy","b":"You do not usually see this bit, so let me show you. Before every single treatment, this is what happens: a proper consultation, a full look at your medical history, a sterile setup, and a plan I am genuinely happy is safe and right for you. That is what nurse-led means. It is not a buzzword on a sign. It is years of clinical training deciding that your safety comes before any treatment, every time. If something is not right for your skin, I will tell you, even if it means you book nothing. That honesty is the whole point of this clinic. Come and experience the difference. Link in bio to book your complimentary analysis. #NurseLedAesthetics #ClinicalSafety #HampshireAesthetics #SkinExpert #BehindTheScenes"}],"2026-09-17#241":[{"h":"What to check","b":"Open Meta Ads Manager and Google Ads and look at the last 7 days. In Meta: cost per lead, cost per result, amount spent, and whether the lead form or booking flow is actually delivering usable enquiries (not just clicks). In Google: cost per click, click-through rate, and whether any 'skin analysis' searches are converting to a booking or call. Cross-check against GHL: how many of this week's leads turned into a booked skin analysis, because that is the number that pays the bills. Note any ad set spending with zero results so it can be paused."},{"h":"What good looks like","b":"Your pre-launch Winchester lead benchmark was about 3.08 pounds each, so on cold prospecting anything roughly 3 to 5 pounds a lead is healthy and under 3 is excellent; drifting past 6 to 7 pounds means the creative is tiring and needs a refresh. But cost per booked analysis matters more than cost per lead: if leads are cheap but almost none book, the offer or the follow-up is the problem, not the ad. On Google, a click-through rate above about 5 percent on branded intent terms is fine early on. If nothing is converting to a booking after a week, check the booking link and the GHL follow-up before spending more."}],"2026-09-18#251":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"Social proof is the strongest converter you have, and a real Bedhampton client saying it was worth it does more than any claim Abi can make about herself. This post uses that borrowed credibility to reassure fence-sitters who have watched the authority and human posts but not yet booked. It sits deliberately before the 25 Sep testimonial-plus-book post so proof builds across the fortnight rather than landing all at once."},{"h":"Creative","b":"A quote-led carousel or a simple Reel with the review on screen. Slide 1 or first frame: the client's words in large clean type on a brand-palette background, hook 'I nearly did not book. I am so glad I did.' If Reel, Abi reads a short intro to camera then the quote appears as an overlay. Absolutely no before-and-after client photos unless you hold written consent; a smiling clothed portrait or a neutral clinic shot is safer. Confirm the client has agreed in writing to their words being shared before this goes out."},{"h":"Copy","b":"This landed in my messages last week and it is exactly why I do this. She came in for the free analysis not expecting much, a little nervous, half sure I would just try to sell her something. Instead we talked honestly about her skin, made a simple plan, and she left knowing what to actually spend her money on. Her words: I felt listened to, not sold to, for the first time. That is the whole clinic in one sentence. If you have been putting off booking because you are not sure what to expect, this is what to expect. Complimentary analysis at Bedhampton until 31 October, link in bio. #ClientLove #NurseLedAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #SkinAnalysis #RealResults"}],"2026-09-20#301":[{"h":"Why","b":"Another five-minute Monday steering check to keep Phase 1 on the rails as the offer deadline gets closer and every booked analysis matters more. With ads live, an email just sent and a full posting calendar, small breakages (a paused ad, a stalled follow-up, an unpublished post) cost real money this close to 31 October. This is your weekly moment to catch them early and schedule the week ahead in one go."},{"h":"How","b":"1) Open the scheduler and confirm this week's posts are queued with correct images, captions and dates, then schedule any not yet in. 2) Load the booking link on your phone and complete a test click through to the calendar. 3) In GHL, check new leads from the past week have received the automated follow-up and that any who enquired have a reply. 4) Glance at Meta and Google for 'Active' status and no rejected ads. 5) Check the skin analysis calendar still has open October slots. 6) Send Abi a one-line 'all good' or a short list of anything that needs her."}],"2026-09-21#311":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"Myth-busting posts perform well because they are useful, shareable and position Abi as the honest expert who cuts through the noise the industry profits from. This one keeps authority high in the back half of the offer window and gives the audience a reason to trust her judgement over an ad or an influencer, which loops straight back to why a personal analysis beats guessing. Shareability also widens reach to new local women without paid spend."},{"h":"Creative","b":"A punchy 15 to 25 second Reel, Abi to camera, fast honest energy. First frame hook text: 'The skincare myth that wastes the most money.' Pick one concrete myth: for example that a higher price always means a better product, or that oily skin should be stripped and dried out, or that you need ten steps. On-screen text reinforcing the myth then the truth. End on a clear 'ask me instead' beat."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Here is a myth that costs people a fortune: that oily skin needs stripping back with harsh products until it feels squeaky clean. It does not. When you strip oily skin, it panics and makes more oil, and now you have breakouts and irritation on top. Your skin is not misbehaving, it is reacting to being attacked. The fix is usually gentler than you think, and it is different for everyone, which is the whole point. Stop guessing from the internet and let someone actually look. Complimentary skin analysis at Bedhampton until 31 October, then 15 percent off whatever we decide together. Link in bio. #SkincareMyths #NurseLedAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #OilySkin #SkinHealth"}],"2026-09-23#331":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"A warm day-in-the-life post keeps the brand feeling human and local in a week between heavier authority and offer posts, giving the feed rhythm rather than relentless selling. It lets the audience picture themselves in the clinic, which lowers the mental barrier to booking. It also gently reinforces that this is a real, busy, cared-for local clinic, not a pop-up, which matters as the Winchester expansion story starts to build."},{"h":"Creative","b":"A montage Reel, 25 to 30 seconds, stitched from the b-roll and day clips filmed on 13 Sep. Hook text: 'A day in my clinic.' Quick warm cuts: unlocking up, the kettle on, laying out the tray, greeting a client, the analysis lamp, writing notes, tidying down, locking up. Abi voiceover over gentle music. Keep it cosy and real, phone-shot, not glossy."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Come and spend a day with me. It starts quietly: kettle on, room set up, everything clean and ready before anyone arrives. Then the day fills with faces, some nervous, some regulars, all of them trusting me with something personal. In between I am reading skin, writing notes, double-checking I am doing right by every single person. It is not glamorous and I would not change a thing. This is the clinic I always wanted to build, and in a few weeks I get to build a second one in Winchester too. If you have never been, come and see what a calm, honest skin appointment actually feels like. Link in bio. #DayInTheClinic #NurseLedAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #WinchesterAesthetics #BedhamptonClinic"}],"2026-09-24#341":[{"h":"What to check","b":"Open Meta Ads Manager and review the trend over the last 7 to 10 days, not just today. Look specifically at cost per lead versus the previous week, frequency (how many times the same person has seen the ad), and CTR. Rising cost per lead plus rising frequency plus falling CTR is the classic signature of creative fatigue: the audience has seen it too often. Also check cost per booked analysis in GHL, since a creative can still be worth keeping if it is quietly driving bookings even as raw lead cost ticks up."},{"h":"What good looks like","b":"Healthy prospecting lead cost sits around the 3.08 pound benchmark, ideally 3 to 5 pounds; if it has crept past roughly 6 pounds and frequency is above about 2.5, refresh the image or video. Swap in one of the newly filmed Reels (the myth or the boosters explainer make strong ad creative) as a fresh ad in the same ad set, keep the winning audience, and let it run 3 to 4 days before judging. Do not touch the audience or budget at the same time, change one thing so you know what worked. If a refresh pulls lead cost back under 5 pounds and bookings hold, it worked."}],"2026-09-25#351":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"This is the fortnight's conversion beat: proof plus a direct ask, timed for the end of September when the 31 October deadline starts to feel real. Pairing a testimonial with the booking call to action combines the two strongest levers, social proof and scarcity, on people who have now seen a fortnight of value. Its job is a spike in booked analyses heading into the final month of the free offer."},{"h":"Creative","b":"A testimonial-forward Reel or carousel ending on a hard booking prompt. Open with a client quote on screen (written consent confirmed), hook 'She waited two years to book. Do not do that.' Then cut to Abi to camera with the offer and the deadline, or a clean closing graphic: 'Free analysis plus 15 percent off, until 31 October.' Make the link and deadline unmissable. Reuse the 25 Sep proof clip from the batch shoot."},{"h":"Copy","b":"One of my clients told me she had wanted to sort her skin for two years before she finally booked, and her only regret was waiting. I hear that a lot. The nerves, the not knowing where to start, the worry it will be pushy: all completely normal, and all gone within about five minutes of sitting down with me. Here is your nudge. The complimentary skin analysis ends on 31 October, and October books up fast, so if you have been meaning to, this is the moment. Twenty honest minutes, a real plan, and 15 percent off anything you choose after. Tap the link in my bio or comment ANALYSIS. #ClientStory #SkinAnalysis #NurseLedAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #BookNow"}],"2026-09-27#401":[{"h":"What to film","b":"Batch the six Reels that shift the story towards Winchester while the Bedhampton offer runs its final month. 1) Winchester teaser, Abi to camera outside or near 9A Jewry Street: 'Something is coming to Winchester high street' (for the 30 Sep human post). 2) Authority piece for 28 Sep, a strong educational to-camera on a treatment or skin concern. 3) Offer reminder, 'analysis closing soon' energy for the 2 Oct post. 4) Countdown-style teaser hinting at the November launch and the Founding Client idea, no discount mentioned. 5) A Winchester local-pride clip, Abi talking about why the city and why now. 6) A spare warm to-camera thank-you or invite. Grab b-roll of Jewry Street, the shopfront if accessible, and Winchester high-street texture."},{"h":"How","b":"Shoot the Winchester exteriors on a bright day with the phone at eye level, framing the street or signage clearly but keeping Abi the focus. Face the light, never shoot into the sun. Each clip 15 to 30 seconds, two takes each, extra footage at the start and end for trims. Record the teasers to feel a little more excited and cinematic than the Bedhampton pieces, since these carry the launch, but keep Abi's natural warm voice. If the shopfront is not ready to show, film Abi near the street with the building out of focus rather than revealing an unfinished unit."}],"2026-09-27#402":[{"h":"Why","b":"Locking the week's posts in one sitting protects the schedule during a busy stretch where the Bedhampton offer is closing and the Winchester story is starting, so two threads are running at once and it is easy to lose one. Scheduling ahead keeps both narratives paced correctly and stops a launch teaser going out before an offer reminder by accident. It also frees Abi from posting admin during a demanding fortnight."},{"h":"How","b":"1) Open the GHL Social Planner. 2) Load the six Reels filmed today onto their planned dates, keeping the offer-close posts and the Winchester teasers correctly sequenced. 3) Paste approved captions, verify booking links and hashtags. 4) Set both Facebook and Instagram as destinations, consistent evening slots. 5) Double-check no launch post reveals a discount for Winchester, since November has none. 6) Confirm every item reads 'Scheduled' and send Abi the week's calendar screenshot for a final sanity check."}],"2026-09-28#411":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"A pure authority post to keep expertise front and centre while the feed also carries offer-close and Winchester-teaser messages, so the brand never tips into feeling like nonstop promotion. Educational content in this window reassures the audience that the clinic leads with knowledge, which is what justifies premium, nurse-led pricing when the no-discount Winchester model arrives. It also gives shareable value that can quietly reach new local women."},{"h":"Creative","b":"A 25 to 30 second to-camera explainer from the 27 Sep batch, Abi in clinic light. Hook frame text: 'The skincare order that actually matters.' Pick a genuinely useful, evergreen topic such as the correct order to apply products, or why SPF is the one non-negotiable, or how long a product really takes to work. Simple on-screen step labels as she talks. End on 'if you want this tailored to your skin, that is what an analysis is for'."},{"h":"Copy","b":"If you take one thing from me this week, make it this: SPF is the single most powerful anti-ageing step you own, and most people skip it or under-use it. Not because they do not care, but because nobody explained that the sun is doing quiet damage even on grey Hampshire days, even through the car window. Every clever serum you buy is working against that damage unless you protect the skin first. A proper daily SPF is not vanity, it is the foundation everything else sits on. Which one and how much depends on your skin, and yes, that is exactly the kind of thing we sort in an analysis. Complimentary at Bedhampton until 31 October, link in bio. #SPF #SkincareBasics #NurseLedAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #SkinHealth"}],"2026-09-29#421":[{"h":"What","b":"Send a deadline-driven email and a short SMS to the warm list reminding them that October is the final month for the free skin analysis and 15 percent off. The goal is a clear, honest urgency push that converts the fence-sitters who have engaged all month but not yet booked. The SMS catches those who skim past emails, and both drive straight to the booking link before the offer closes on 31 October."},{"h":"Copy","b":"EMAIL\nSubject: October is the last month for your free skin analysis\n\nHi {name},\n\nA quick and honest heads-up. On 31 October, the complimentary skin analysis at my Bedhampton clinic comes to an end, and so does the 15 percent off any treatment you book afterwards.\n\nIf you have been meaning to book and life has got in the way, this is your reminder. It is 20 minutes, no pressure to book anything on the day, just a proper expert look at your skin and a plan that actually fits it. October always fills up faster than people expect, so the earlier you grab a slot, the better the choice of times.\n\nBook your slot here: {link}\n\nAnd if now is not the moment, no worries at all, I will still be here.\n\nWarmly,\nAbi\nRegistered Nurse and founder, Abi Peters Skin Clinic\n\nSMS\nHi {name}, it is Abi. Just a heads-up that October is the final month for a free skin analysis plus 15 percent off at my Bedhampton clinic. 20 honest minutes, no pressure. Grab a slot before they go: {link}"}],"2026-09-30#431":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"This is the first proper Winchester reveal to the public feed, turning the warm 443-lead list's expectation into a shared public moment and building anticipation for the 2 November launch. It deliberately trades on local pride: a nurse-led skin clinic coming to Winchester high street is genuine local news. It seeds the Founding Client idea without naming a discount, so the launch keeps its premium, no-sale positioning while still feeling exciting and exclusive."},{"h":"Creative","b":"The Winchester teaser Reel from the 27 Sep batch, Abi near 9A Jewry Street. Hook frame text: 'Winchester, I have something to tell you.' Show the street, a hint of the location, Abi genuinely excited but warm. B-roll of Winchester high street, the building exterior if presentable. End on a date card: 'Opening 2 November' and a soft 'founding clients, watch this space'. Keep the shopfront reveal partial if the unit is not finished."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Winchester, this one is for you. On 2 November, Abi Peters Skin Clinic opens its doors on Jewry Street, right in the heart of the city. Same honest, nurse-led skin care I have built in Bedhampton, now on your high street. I have wanted to bring this to Winchester for a long time, and it still does not feel quite real to say it out loud. I am opening with a small group of founding clients who will get something special from the very start, and I will share exactly what that means very soon. If you want to be first to hear, make sure you are following and keep an eye on your messages. See you in November. #WinchesterAesthetics #JewryStreet #HampshireAesthetics #NurseLedAesthetics #ComingSoon"}],"2026-10-01#441":[{"h":"What to check","b":"Start-of-October review across Meta, Google and GHL for the last 7 to 14 days. In Meta and Google: spend, cost per lead, cost per click, CTR and whether ads are still Active and approved. In GHL: how many leads became booked skin analyses this month, and how many of those analyses have converted to a paid treatment, since the 15 percent offer's real payoff is treatments booked. Also sanity-check the total spend for September against bookings generated so you know the true cost per booked analysis, not just cost per lead."},{"h":"What good looks like","b":"Lead cost near the 3.08 pound benchmark is healthy; 3 to 5 pounds is fine, over 6 to 7 signals a refresh is due. The number that actually matters is cost per booked analysis: even at, say, 15 to 25 pounds per booked analysis, if a decent share of those book a treatment at premium prices the maths works comfortably. If leads are cheap but bookings are thin, the bottleneck is the follow-up or the booking friction, so check the GHL automation and the link before adding budget. With the offer closing this month, prioritise converting existing leads over chasing new cheap ones."}],"2026-10-02#451":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"The final-month closing push on the offer: a clear, urgent, honest reminder that the free analysis ends on 31 October, aimed at converting everyone who has warmed up over the whole of September but not yet acted. This is deadline scarcity used truthfully, and it is the last big organic-social nudge before the offer disappears. It also cleanly hands the audience's attention over to the Winchester launch story that now takes centre stage."},{"h":"Creative","b":"A direct, confident Reel or static using the offer-reminder clip from the 27 Sep batch. Hook frame text: 'Last chance: free skin analysis ends this month.' If Reel, Abi to camera with warm urgency and the deadline; if static, a clean brand-palette graphic reading 'Complimentary Skin Analysis plus 15 percent off, ends 31 October' with the booking link prominent. Keep the call to action single and obvious: book, or comment ANALYSIS."},{"h":"Copy","b":"This is the last call, said plainly. The complimentary skin analysis at my Bedhampton clinic ends on 31 October, and with it the 15 percent off anything you book afterwards. After that, it is gone. If you have followed along this month, learned a bit, and thought I really should book that, this is the moment to actually do it. Twenty honest minutes, a real plan for your skin, zero pressure to book a treatment on the day. The remaining October slots are filling now, so do not leave it to the last week. Tap the link in my bio or comment ANALYSIS and I will send it straight over. #LastChance #SkinAnalysis #NurseLedAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #BedhamptonClinic"}],"2026-10-04#501":[{"h":"Why","b":"This is your weekly control-tower moment, the Sunday reset that stops small things becoming last-minute panics. With the Bedhampton offer closing on 31 October and the Winchester launch four weeks out, 30 minutes now keeps every plate spinning. You confirm what shipped, what slipped, and what needs Abi before the week runs away."},{"h":"How","b":"1. Open your GHL dashboard and count last week's booked skin analyses. Write the number down so you can see the trend week to week. 2. In Meta Ads Manager, glance at spend and cost per lead (aim near 3.08 pounds). 3. Open the content planner and confirm every post for 5 to 12 Oct has copy, an image or Reel, and a scheduled time. 4. Check the Winchester Google Business Profile reconnection status; if it is verified, add 'fill out profile' to Friday. 5. Message Abi one line: which day she can film this week, plus any clips she can grab between clients. 6. Block 30 minutes next Sunday for the same check-in."}],"2026-10-05#511":[{"h":"What","b":"This email opens Founding Client booking to the 443 warm Winchester leads, before anyone else hears about it. The goal is to fill as many of the 40 capped spots as possible using honest scarcity and a genuine head start, not a discount. Every reader put their hand up months ago, so the tone is 'you first', not 'buy now'."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Subject: Winchester, your Founding Client invitation is open\n\nHi {name},\n\nI have news I have been dying to share. On 2 November I open my second clinic, right in the heart of Winchester at 9A Jewry Street.\n\nYou are on this list because you put your hand up months ago, when Winchester was still just a plan on paper. So before I tell anyone else, I want to give you first refusal on something special.\n\nThe first 40 people become Founding Clients. That means:\n\n1. Priority booking, so you get the first and best appointment slots.\n2. A complimentary skincare add-on with your first treatment.\n3. Founding pricing, locked in for good.\n\nThere is no discount, because this is not a sale. It is a thank you to the people who believed in this before the paint was dry, and a real head start that stays with you for as long as you are my client.\n\nSpots are capped at 40, and this list is longer than that, so they will go.\n\nClaim your Founding Client spot: {link}\n\nI cannot wait to see you in Winchester.\n\nAbi\nRegistered Nurse, Abi Peters Skin Clinic"}],"2026-10-05#512":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"The first authority post of the Winchester run-up. It resets who Abi is in the feed: not a beauty brand, a nurse. Authority posts build the clinical trust that makes the later Founding offer and launch land as safe rather than salesy, and they gently warm the 443 leads who followed but have not yet booked."},{"h":"Creative","b":"20 to 30 second Reel, Abi to camera in the clinic, natural light, phone at eye level. On-screen hook text: \"'Nurse-led' is not a marketing word.\" Keep the delivery calm and unscripted. Cut in b-roll over the talking: hands washing, gloves going on, a considered look at skin under proper light."},{"h":"Copy","b":"'Nurse-led' is not a buzzword. It is who is holding the needle. I am a registered nurse, which means before anything touches your skin I have assessed your health, your history and what your face actually needs. It means if something ever needs managing, I can manage it. Aesthetics is medical, even when it looks like beauty, and I think you deserve someone who trained for the medical bit. Booking a skin analysis is the easiest way to meet me first. Tap the link or comment HELLO. #NurseLedAesthetics #WinchesterAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #SkinHealth #SafeAesthetics"}],"2026-10-06#521":[{"h":"Objective","b":"Switch the running Bedhampton campaign from a lead-form objective to a Conversions objective optimised for the booked appointment event, the analysis booking confirmation or the GHL 'booked' event. You are telling Meta to find people who actually book, not just people who fill in a form. Keep it live until 31 October, then it pauses on its own."},{"h":"Audience","b":"Two ad sets. Set A, retargeting: everyone who engaged with your Facebook or Instagram in the last 90 days plus the warm lead list, women, 30 to 55. Set B, cold: women 30 to 55 within a 10 mile radius of Bedhampton and Havant, with skincare and beauty interests. Exclude anyone who has already booked, so you are not paying to reach them twice."},{"h":"Budget","b":"Start at 15 pounds a day, split across the two ad sets. That is enough to gather real booking data through October without burning through the budget you will want for Winchester. If cost per booked analysis holds under 20 pounds, you can lift it to 20 for the final week."},{"h":"Creative","b":"A 20 to 30 second clip of Abi talking straight to camera about the free analysis, warm and unscripted, ending on 'comment ANALYSIS or tap the link'. Add one clean static image of the Bedhampton clinic as a second creative in the same ad set, so Meta can test video against still and pick the winner."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Primary text: Before you spend another penny on skincare, come and find out what your skin actually needs. A complimentary 20 minute skin analysis with a registered nurse at my Bedhampton clinic, plus 15 percent off anything you book after. No pressure, just honest advice. Closes 31 October. Headline: Free nurse-led skin analysis"}],"2026-10-07#531":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"The first human, behind-the-scenes post of the build. Its job is emotional buy-in: people follow the person and the story, not the premises. Documenting the empty Winchester space starts a narrative that pays off at launch and makes local followers feel they were there from the bare-walls beginning."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Reel or carousel. Film a slow pan across the empty room, then Abi standing in the middle of it, then a short piece to camera. On-screen hook: \"This is what a dream looks like before the paint.\" If you go carousel: bare room, Abi in the space, a close-up of the 9A door number on Jewry Street."},{"h":"Copy","b":"This is what a dream looks like with bare walls and a concrete floor. 9A Jewry Street, right in the middle of Winchester, and in a few weeks it becomes my second home. I keep standing in the empty room picturing where the chair goes, where you will sit with a cup of tea while we talk about your skin. Bedhampton will always be home base, but Winchester has had my heart for a long time. Follow along, I am documenting all of it. #Winchester #JewryStreet #WinchesterHighStreet #NurseLedAesthetics #ComingSoon"}],"2026-10-09#551":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"The one overtly offer-led post this week, placed to do two jobs at once: create urgency on the closing Bedhampton analysis for revenue now, and seed the Winchester Founding scarcity for revenue later. Splitting the call to action by keyword lets you track which offer each person actually wants."},{"h":"Creative","b":"A clean carousel, chosen for clarity because it carries two offers. Slide 1: bold text 'Two things closing soon'. Slide 2: Bedhampton free analysis plus 15 percent off, ends 31 October. Slide 3: Winchester Founding Clients, first 40 only. Keep the design simple and on-brand so the two offers never blur together."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Two things worth knowing this week. One: the free skin analysis at my Bedhampton clinic, plus 15 percent off anything you book after it, closes on 31 October. If you have been meaning to book, this is your nudge. Two: Winchester is opening, and the first 40 Founding Clients get priority booking, a complimentary skincare add-on with their first treatment, and their pricing locked in for good. No discount, something better: a place at the front of the queue. Comment FOUNDING for Winchester or ANALYSIS for Bedhampton and I will send the link. #WinchesterAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #FoundingClient #SkinAnalysis #NurseLed"}],"2026-10-11#601":[{"h":"What to film","b":"Batch six clips in one sitting, each feeding a named post over the next two weeks. 1. Skin boosters explainer, to camera, 30 seconds, for the 12 Oct authority post: 'A skin booster is not filler.' 2. Fit-out walkthrough, a slow pan of the room plus the frontage or sign, b-roll with no talking, for the 14 Oct human post. 3. Autumn timing, to camera, for the 19 Oct authority post: 'Autumn is the best time to start.' 4. A warm 'why Winchester' piece to camera, for the 21 Oct countdown post. 5. B-roll bank: hands prepping, the kettle, tea being poured, products lined up, for the 16 and 23 Oct proof posts. 6. A short Founding Client explainer to camera: first 40, priority booking, add-on, pricing locked in, for offer content."},{"h":"How","b":"Film near a window in daylight, phone at eye level on anything steady, and keep each clip 15 to 30 seconds. Film every clip two or three times and shoot far more b-roll than you think you need; it is much easier to cut than to reshoot. Speak like you are telling a friend, leave a beat of silence at the start and end of each take, and keep one clean version with no on-screen text so David can add captions later."}],"2026-10-11#602":[{"h":"Why","b":"Filming is wasted if it sits on Abi's phone. Turning the six new Reels into scheduled posts guarantees a steady drumbeat through the run-up to launch, with no daily scramble to find something to post. A filled calendar also lets you see gaps and fix them now rather than at 9pm on a posting day."},{"h":"How","b":"1. Collect the six clips from Abi via AirDrop or a shared folder. 2. In the GHL Social Planner (or Meta Business Suite), create a draft for each. 3. Paste the matching caption from this plan onto each clip. 4. Set dates across the next two weeks: authority around Mon, human midweek, offer or proof toward Fri. 5. Set every post to publish to both Facebook and Instagram. 6. Put hashtags in the first comment if you want a clean caption. 7. Schedule them, screenshot the filled calendar, and send it to Abi so she knows exactly what is going out and when."}],"2026-10-12#611":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"An authority post that doubles as gentle product education, lowering the barrier for people who are curious but confused about treatments. Clearing up the most common mix-up positions Abi as the honest explainer and nudges analysis bookings from people who did not realise a booster was an option for them."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Reel, Abi to camera, natural light. On-screen hook: 'Skin boosters are NOT filler.' Use your hands to gesture the difference between adding shape and adding hydration. B-roll to cut in: a hydrating serum, a glass of water, a close-up of healthy skin catching the light."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Let me clear up the most common mix-up I hear. A skin booster is not filler. Filler adds shape. A skin booster works underneath, giving your skin a deep drink of hydration so it looks healthier, bouncier and more like your skin on its best day. No new shape, no puffiness, just glow that comes from within the skin itself. It is one of my favourite treatments for people who want to look well rather than done. Want to know if your skin would suit one? Book an analysis and we will look together. #SkinBoosters #NurseLedAesthetics #WinchesterAesthetics #HampshireSkin #NaturalResults"}],"2026-10-13#621":[{"h":"Why","b":"A verified Google Business Profile is how people find you when they search 'skin clinic Winchester' or tap the map. An empty or bare profile quietly loses you to competitors who filled theirs in. Now that the Winchester listing is verified, completing it properly is one of the highest-return hours you will spend all month, and the press release puts your opening in front of local readers for free."},{"h":"How","b":"1. In Google Business Profile, open the Winchester listing. 2. Set the address to 9A Jewry Street, choose the primary category 'Skin care clinic' and add 'Medical spa' and 'Nurse', and set opening hours (add the 2 November open date if the field allows). 3. Upload at least eight photos: frontage, treatment room, Abi, logo, interior. 4. Publish the first Google post: 'Now opening on Jewry Street, 2 November. Nurse-led skin care in the heart of Winchester,' with the booking link. 5. Add the booking link and phone number. 6. Turn on messaging only if you will monitor it. 7. Email the press release to Hampshire Chronicle, Winchester News Extra and local what's-on and community Facebook groups, with two photos attached."}],"2026-10-14#631":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"The second fit-out human post, moving the build story on from empty room to named frontage. A visible sign makes the launch feel real and imminent, and leaning into Jewry Street pride pulls in the local Winchester followers who will become walk-past, walk-in clients."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Reel. Film the sign going up, or the finished frontage, then Abi sitting across the road looking at it with a coffee. On-screen hook: 'My name just went up on Jewry Street.' Shoot in bright daylight or golden hour so the frontage reads clearly."},{"h":"Copy","b":"There is a moment that makes it real, and for me it was watching the signage go up on Jewry Street. Years of being the nurse people quietly recommend to their friends, and now there is my name, in the middle of Winchester, for everyone to see. I sat across the road with a coffee and just looked at it for a while. Opening day is 2 November. If you are local, give me a wave when you walk past. #Winchester #WinchesterHighStreet #JewryStreet #NurseLedAesthetics #WinchesterLife"}],"2026-10-15#641":[{"h":"What to check","b":"Open Meta Ads Manager and look past the vanity numbers. Check cost per booked analysis, not just cost per lead, by comparing spend against the bookings showing in GHL. Note the click-through rate, the frequency (how many times the same person has seen the ad), and which ad set and creative is winning. Then check GHL for how many booked analyses actually showed up, because a booking that no-shows is not a result."},{"h":"What good looks like","b":"The pre-launch lead cost was about 3.08 pounds, but cost per booked analysis is the number that matters now. For a premium clinic where a happy client is worth hundreds over time, a booked analysis under roughly 15 to 20 pounds is healthy. Aim for a click-through rate above 1 percent and keep frequency under about 2.5 over a two week window. If frequency is climbing while click-through and bookings fall, the creative is fatiguing: swap in a fresh clip before you spend more."}],"2026-10-16#651":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"The first proof post. Social proof converts the warm-but-hesitant, and the specific fear that stops people booking an analysis is that it will be a hard sell. A real client quote about how low-pressure and useful it was answers that fear directly, which is more persuasive than any claim Abi could make about herself."},{"h":"Creative","b":"A clean quote card, or a Reel of Abi reading the quote to camera. On-screen hook: the quote itself, large and simple. If Reel, cut b-roll of the consultation setting behind her voice. Do not show any client's face, or any before-and-after image, without written consent; this post uses words only."},{"h":"Copy","b":"I asked a client if I could share what she said after her skin analysis, and she said yes. 'I have spent years buying products off the internet and hoping. Twenty minutes with Abi and I finally understood my own skin.' That is the whole point. Not selling you the treatment an ad wants you to want, but telling you honestly what your skin actually needs, and sometimes that is less than you think. The free analysis at Bedhampton is open until 31 October. Tap the link or comment ANALYSIS. #SkinAnalysis #NurseLedAesthetics #WinchesterAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #HonestSkincare"}],"2026-10-18#701":[{"h":"Why","b":"The second weekly control-tower moment, now just over two weeks from launch and just under two weeks from the Bedhampton offer closing. This is where you catch anything drifting: unfilled Founding spots, a countdown post not yet drafted, a booking link that still points at the wrong page. A calm 30 minutes here buys a calm launch week."},{"h":"How","b":"1. In GHL, check how many Founding Client spots are claimed against the cap of 40, and note the number so this week's copy stays honest. 2. Count analysis bookings still coming in before the 31 Oct close; if slow, plan a boost. 3. Confirm the countdown posts (26, 28, 30 Oct) and the opening post are drafted or scheduled. 4. Confirm Abi's grand-opening film day is locked for 25 Oct. 5. Check the Winchester Google profile is live with photos and hours. 6. Confirm the final analysis-push email is queued for 20 Oct. 7. Send Abi a two-line summary of where things stand."}],"2026-10-19#711":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"A timely authority post that uses the season itself as the hook. It reframes autumn as the smart time to act, giving fence-sitters a concrete reason to book now rather than 'someday', and it lands just before the Bedhampton offer's final week to push those last bookings."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Reel, Abi to camera, ideally with warm autumn light or a cosy clinic feel. On-screen hook: 'Autumn is the best-kept secret in skincare.' Cut in b-roll of warm tones, a mug of tea, and a diary or plan being written, to reinforce the 'make a plan' message."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Here is something I wish more people knew. Autumn is one of the best times of year to start looking after your skin properly. The strong summer sun has eased off, which makes it a kinder window to begin certain treatments, and it gives your skin time to settle and glow before Christmas. Skin loves a plan, not a panic in December. If you have put your skin at the bottom of the list all summer, this is the season to move it up. Book a skin analysis and let us make a plan together. #SkinHealth #NurseLedAesthetics #WinchesterAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #AutumnSkincare"}],"2026-10-20#721":[{"h":"What","b":"The final push email to the whole list, driving the last wave of bookings for the Bedhampton free analysis and 15 percent off before both close on 31 October. The goal is to convert the people who have been meaning to book and keep forgetting, using a clear deadline and a reminder of how genuinely low-pressure the analysis is."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Subject: Your free skin analysis closes on 31 October\n\nHi {name},\n\nA quick and honest heads-up: the complimentary skin analysis at my Bedhampton clinic finishes on 31 October, and so does the 15 percent off anything you book afterwards.\n\nIf you have been meaning to book and life got in the way, I completely understand. But I do not want you to miss it.\n\nHere is what the analysis actually is. Twenty minutes, just the two of us, your skin under proper clinical light. I tell you what is really going on and what your skin genuinely needs, not what an advert wants to sell you. There is zero pressure to book a single treatment, and plenty of people leave with a simple plan and nothing to buy.\n\nI have a limited number of appointments left before the end of the month.\n\nBook your free analysis: {link}\n\nWarmly,\nAbi\nRegistered Nurse, Abi Peters Skin Clinic"}],"2026-10-21#731":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"A human countdown post at roughly twelve days out. Its job is emotional momentum and honest scarcity: sharing the real feelings of nervous-and-excited deepens the bond that turns followers into first-week clients, while a light mention of filling Founding spots keeps quiet pressure on without ever tipping into hype."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Selfie-style Reel, Abi talking honestly to camera, phone at eye level, natural light. On-screen hook: '12 days. I keep saying it out loud.' Intercut two or three quick build clips (the room, the sign) so the words are backed by visible progress."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Twelve days until I open the doors in Winchester and honestly, I feel all of it. Excited, proud, a little bit terrified, the way you should feel when something matters. People ask why Winchester, and the truth is simple: I have loved this city for years and I wanted to bring careful, honest, nurse-led skin care right into the heart of it. The Founding Client spots are filling, the room is nearly ready, and I cannot wait to meet you properly. 2 November. Jewry Street. See you there. #Winchester #WinchesterHighStreet #NurseLedAesthetics #FoundingClient #Countdown"}],"2026-10-22#741":[{"h":"Why","b":"Launch week is not the time to be writing captions or working out how the Founding offer redeems. Finalising it all now, while things are calm, means launch week runs itself and Abi can be present on the clinic floor. It also makes the Founding offer airtight: exactly what is included, how many spots are left, and precisely how a client claims one."},{"h":"How","b":"1. Sit with Abi for an hour and lock the Founding bundle wording word for word: first 40, priority booking, complimentary skincare add-on with the first treatment, founding pricing locked in for good. 2. Check the GHL booking flow tags a Founding Client and stops accepting them at 40. 3. Read every drafted launch-week post (countdowns, grand-opening Reel caption, opening-day post) for tone and accuracy. 4. Confirm the dates: countdowns on 26, 28 and 30 Oct, opening post on 2 Nov. 5. Note how many Founding spots remain so the copy stays honest. 6. Agree who replies to comments and DMs each day of launch week."}],"2026-10-23#751":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"A final-week post that combines proof with a clean last-chance deadline for the Bedhampton offer. Reassurance plus a real cut-off (31 Oct) converts the last wave of hesitant bookers before the whole story turns to Winchester, and it keeps the offer honest: it genuinely ends, which is why people act."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Reel or static. On-screen hook: 'Last chance, and I mean it kindly.' If Reel, Abi to camera with a warm, calm delivery; if static, a simple text card with the 31 October date front and centre. B-roll of the analysis light and the consultation setting works well underneath."},{"h":"Copy","b":"A gentle last call. The free skin analysis at my Bedhampton clinic, and the 15 percent off you get on anything you book after it, both stop on 31 October. I will not be running it again in the same way once Winchester opens, so if it has been sitting on your to-do list, this is the moment. Twenty minutes, proper light, honest advice, no pressure to book a single thing. That is all it is. Comment ANALYSIS or tap the link and I will get you in before it closes. #SkinAnalysis #LastChance #NurseLedAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #BedhamptonClinic"}],"2026-10-25#801":[{"h":"Why","b":"During launch week Abi is on the clinic floor, not on her phone. Pre-scheduling every countdown post and the opening-day email and text now means the marketing fires on time, to the second, without anyone having to remember it on the day. It also gives you time to test every link before it matters."},{"h":"How","b":"1. In the social planner, schedule the countdown posts for 26, 28 and 30 Oct, and the opening post for the morning of 2 Nov, both platforms. 2. Attach the grand-opening Reel to the 2 Nov post once Abi sends it. 3. In GHL, build the launch email (subject: We are open, Winchester) and schedule it for 2 Nov at 8am to the whole list. 4. Build the launch SMS for 2 Nov to the warm Winchester segment: 'We are open. Nurse-led skin care is now on Jewry Street, Winchester. Book your first appointment: {link} Abi x'. 5. Send yourself a preview of both. 6. Click every link and confirm it lands on the live Winchester booking page. 7. Screenshot the full schedule for Abi."}],"2026-10-25#802":[{"h":"What to film","b":"This is the hero Reel for launch, so shoot it as a sequence. 1. Exterior: Abi walking up to 9A Jewry Street and opening the door. 2. The reveal: pushing the door open into the finished room from her point of view. 3. Detail shots: the treatment chair, the sign, a candle, tea being poured, products lined up neatly. 4. Abi to camera, a short warm welcome: 'We are open, Winchester.' 5. A slow turn showing the whole finished room. 6. End frame: Abi by the door, a real smile, 'See you on Jewry Street,' ready for an open-date text overlay."},{"h":"How","b":"Film on the brightest part of the day with as much natural light as possible, phone at eye level and steady. Keep each shot 15 to 30 seconds and film every one two or three times so you have options in the edit. Shoot far more b-roll than you think you need, and record at least one clean take of every talking shot with no on-screen text so David can add captions. Move slowly; slow, steady footage always looks more premium than rushed."}],"2026-10-26#811":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"The one-week countdown post. Pure momentum, plus the strongest remaining Founding-spot push while spots are still available. It is aimed squarely at the warm leads who have watched the whole build and just need one final, specific nudge to claim their place before opening."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Reel. On-screen hook: '1 week.' A short montage of the finished room set to calm, upbeat audio, ending on Abi. Alternatively a bold '7 days to go' text card over a slow clip of the treatment room. Keep it clean and proud rather than loud."},{"h":"Copy","b":"One week. Seven days until 9A Jewry Street opens and Winchester gets its own nurse-led skin clinic. I have gone from bare walls to a room I am genuinely proud of, and I cannot wait to show you inside. If you want to be among the very first through the door, the Founding Client list is nearly full: priority booking, a complimentary skincare add-on with your first treatment, and your pricing locked in for good. Comment FOUNDING and I will send you the details before the spots go. #Winchester #WinchesterHighStreet #FoundingClient #NurseLedAesthetics #OneWeekToGo"}],"2026-10-27#821":[{"h":"Objective","b":"A Conversions campaign optimised for bookings, plus a small awareness layer for reach around opening week, mirrored on Google with a Search campaign so people typing 'aesthetics Winchester' find you. Build everything now, set every campaign to Paused, and only switch live on launch morning."},{"h":"Audience","b":"On Meta, two ad sets: cold, women 30 to 55 within an 8 mile radius of Winchester (SO23); and retargeting, the 443 warm Winchester leads plus your page and profile engagers. Keep them separate so you can read each one. On Google, target the Winchester area with keywords like 'skin clinic Winchester', 'aesthetics Winchester' and 'skin consultation Winchester'."},{"h":"Budget","b":"20 pounds a day on Meta for launch week and 10 pounds a day on Google Search. Review after the first three days and move budget toward whichever is producing booked appointments, not just clicks. Google search intent often books cheaper for a local clinic, so be ready to shift toward it if it performs."},{"h":"Creative","b":"The grand-opening Reel as the primary Meta creative, plus a clean static of the Jewry Street frontage as a second option. Google Search needs no image, it is text: your strongest headlines about nurse-led skin care now open in Winchester. Do not name any prescription treatment in any of these ads."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Meta primary text: Winchester, your high street just gained a nurse-led skin clinic. Honest advice, natural results, and a registered nurse who treats skin health as medical care. Now open at 9A Jewry Street. Book your first appointment today. Meta headline: Nurse-led skin care, now in Winchester. Google headlines: 'Nurse-Led Skin Clinic Winchester', 'Aesthetics On Jewry Street', 'Book A Skin Consultation'. Google description: Registered nurse, natural results, honest advice. Now open on Winchester high street. Book online today."}],"2026-10-28#831":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"The five-day countdown, leaning into cosy, human detail rather than hype. Small touches (the chair in, the kettle on) keep the tone like a friend sharing progress, while an honest note that Founding spots are nearly gone maintains gentle urgency for the last few undecided leads."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Reel of the finished treatment room, a slow reveal of the chair, then the kettle, then Abi. On-screen hook: 'The chair is in. The kettle is on.' Warm light and unhurried camera movement to keep it feeling premium and personal."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Five days. The treatment room is finished, the chair is in, and yes, the kettle is already on because you know I run on tea. It still makes me a little emotional walking in and seeing it all come together on Jewry Street. A handful of Founding Client spots are left before we open on 2 November, and once they are gone, they are gone. If you have been waiting for a sign, this is it. Comment FOUNDING or tap the link. #Winchester #WinchesterHighStreet #NurseLedAesthetics #FoundingClient #Countdown"}],"2026-10-29#841":[{"h":"Why","b":"A soft launch is a dress rehearsal. Treating friends, family and a few Founding Clients across 30 October to 1 November lets you test the room, the flow, the kettle, the booking system and any nerves before the public opening on 2 November. Real people in real chairs surface problems while they are still cheap and easy to fix."},{"h":"How","b":"1. Pick 8 to 12 friendly faces: family, a couple of loyal Bedhampton clients, and three or four keen Founding Clients. 2. Message each one personally, not as a broadcast, inviting them to a soft-open slot between 30 Oct and 1 Nov. 3. Space the appointments so you can run each one properly and notice what feels off. 4. Ask everyone to bring honest feedback on parking, signage, comfort and the warmth of the welcome. 5. Ask permission to film or photograph, and get it in writing from anyone whose face or results you might post. 6. Have the real booking system live so you test the actual flow, not a pretend one."}],"2026-10-30#851":[{"h":"Why","b":"Today the room stops being a plan and becomes a clinic. The goal is not sales, it is smoothness: does the welcome feel right, does the booking flow work, does the space say what you want it to say. Every wrinkle you find today is one your first paying client on 2 November will never notice."},{"h":"How","b":"1. Arrive early and set the room: lighting, temperature, music low, kettle on. 2. Run each soft-launch guest exactly as you would a paying client, from welcome to goodbye. 3. Keep a notepad by the door and jot every friction point the moment it happens. 4. Capture content only with written consent: hands at work, the frontage, a happy guest if they agree. 5. At the end of the day, walk the room with David and fix the top three issues. 6. Post one quiet story, not a full reveal, so followers feel the countdown building without seeing everything before opening day."}],"2026-10-30#852":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"The final countdown post before opening, three days out. Maximum warmth and gratitude, with one last light Founding nudge. This post should feel like a friend about to do the biggest thing of her year, because that emotional peak is what makes people show up and share it on."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Selfie Reel, Abi to camera, genuine and a little emotional, phone at eye level. On-screen hook: '3 sleeps.' Optionally end on a quick flash of the finished room so the feeling is matched to the reveal, without giving the whole space away before opening day."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Three sleeps until we open. I have checked the room about forty times, practised saying 'welcome to Winchester' in the mirror, and tried very hard to stay calm. On 2 November the doors of 9A Jewry Street open and I get to do the thing I love in a city I adore. Thank you to everyone who has followed this build from bare walls to this. If you have not grabbed a Founding Client spot yet, there are only a few left. Comment FOUNDING. See you very soon. #Winchester #WinchesterHighStreet #NurseLedAesthetics #FoundingClient #AlmostThere"}],"2026-10-31#861":[{"h":"Why","b":"Today the Bedhampton offer closes. The job is to convert the last wave of interest and to close the loop cleanly, so from tomorrow the whole story becomes Winchester. A clear, real ending also keeps the offer honest: it genuinely ends, which is exactly why people acted on it in the first place."},{"h":"How","b":"1. Post one final story and feed reminder this morning: 'Last day for the free analysis and 15 percent off,' with the link in bio. 2. Reply to every ANALYSIS comment and DM today and get them booked before midnight. 3. In GHL and Meta, confirm the offer's ad set and any auto-replies switch off at end of day, so nothing promises it in November. 4. Check no scheduled November post still mentions the Bedhampton offer. 5. Move every new analysis lead into the nurture list so they hear about Winchester next. 6. Tell Abi the final tally of analyses booked, then close the tab. Winchester starts tomorrow."}],"2026-11-01#901":[{"h":"Why","b":"Tomorrow's email is the one message that reaches all 443 warm Winchester leads plus the full client list at the exact moment the doors open. If it is not scheduled correctly, or it fires with a dead booking link, you lose the single biggest spike of attention you will get all year. Ten quiet minutes checking it tonight protects months of lead spend."},{"h":"How","b":"1. Log in to GHL, then left menu, Marketing, then Emails, then the Scheduled or Campaigns tab. 2. Open the launch email (task 912) and confirm the send date reads 2 Nov and the time is what you agreed, ideally 7am so it lands before the working day. 3. Confirm the recipient smart list includes both the 443 Winchester lead tag and the full client list, and check the count looks like several hundred, not a handful. 4. Send a test to accounts@abipetersaesthetics.co.uk, open it on your phone, and tap every link. The book button must open the live Winchester booking page, not a 404. 5. Check the launch SMS is scheduled too, and that {name} and {link} resolve in the test. 6. Confirm the wording says Founding Client, with no mention of a discount, then leave it scheduled. Do not send early."}],"2026-11-02#911":[{"h":"Why","b":"This is the day the whole plan has been building towards. Everything today either compounds into reviews, bookings and content you will use for months, or it quietly slips past. A calm running order means you capture the moment instead of just living through it in a blur."},{"h":"How","b":"1. Before doors open, film the empty, finished clinic: the sign at 9A Jewry Street, the treatment room, reception. This is b-roll for weeks of content, so shoot far more than feels necessary. 2. Confirm the launch email and text actually sent (GHL, Marketing, Emails, Sent) and that the ads flipped live (task 914). 3. Have the Google review QR code printed on the desk and the direct review link saved in your phone. 4. Greet every client warmly, mention the Founding Client bundle in person to anyone who has not treated yet, and note who is happy with a photo or a few words. 5. Post the grand-opening Reel and run stories through the day (task 913). 6. At close, jot down walk-ins, bookings taken, reviews left and consented photos captured. That is your launch scorecard for the week."}],"2026-11-02#912":[{"h":"What","b":"The single announcement that the Winchester clinic on Jewry Street is open, sent to the 443 warm leads and the full list the moment the doors open. The goal is to turn warm intent into booked Founding Client places while only 40 exist. This is not a discount message: the urgency is the genuine cap of 40, never a price cut."},{"h":"Copy","b":"SUBJECT: The Winchester clinic is open (and there are only 40 of these)\n\nHi {name},\n\nIt is real. The doors at 9A Jewry Street are open, and I could not be more proud to bring nurse-led skin care to the heart of Winchester.\n\nIf you have been following along, thank you for waiting. Here is the part I saved for you.\n\nThe first 40 people to book become Founding Clients. That means priority booking whenever you want it, a complimentary skincare add-on with your first treatment, and your founding pricing locked in for good. Not for a launch week. For as long as you are my client.\n\nThere is no discount code and no gimmick. Just a proper thank you to the people who trusted a brand new clinic first, and an honest cap at 40.\n\nEvery treatment still starts the way it should: I actually look at your skin and tell you what it needs, honestly.\n\nTap below to claim a Founding Client place before they are gone.\n{link}\n\nSee you on Jewry Street,\nAbi\nRegistered nurse and founder, Abi Peters Skin Clinic\n\nSMS: Hi {name}, it is Abi. The Winchester clinic on Jewry Street is officially open. The first 40 to book are Founding Clients: priority booking, a free skincare add-on with your first treatment, and founding pricing locked in for good. No discount, just a real thank you and a genuine cap of 40. Claim your place: {link}"}],"2026-11-02#913":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"The launch email and ads drive intent; this Reel gives that intent a face and a feeling. It is the emotional proof that a real nurse opened a real clinic in Winchester today, which is what turns a scroll into a save and a booking. It also gives the 443 leads who never opened the email a second, more human way in."},{"h":"Creative","b":"A 20 to 30 second Reel. Open on you opening the door of 9A Jewry Street, then quick warm cuts: the sign, walking into the treatment room, a hand on the chair, a smile to camera. On-screen hook on the first clip: \"We're open, Winchester.\" Shoot in natural window light, phone at eye level, and grab extra b-roll of reception and the street. Run the story series alongside it through the day: doors open, first client (with consent), a close-up of the Founding Client card, and a frame reading \"40 places, this is what one looks like\" with the link sticker."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Winchester, I have keys to a door on Jewry Street and I still cannot quite believe it.\n\nAs of today, nurse-led skin care has a home right in the middle of town. No hard sell, no one-size-fits-all. Just me, actually looking at your skin and telling you the truth about what it needs.\n\nTo thank the people backing a brand new clinic, the first 40 to book become Founding Clients: priority booking, a complimentary skincare add-on with your first treatment, and founding pricing locked in for good. When 40 is gone, it is gone.\n\nTap the link in my bio to claim a place, or come and say hello on Jewry Street.\n\n#WinchesterAesthetics #JewryStreet #HampshireSkinClinic #NurseLedAesthetics #WinchesterHighStreet #FoundingClient"}],"2026-11-02#914":[{"h":"Why","b":"The ads are the engine that keeps new Winchester strangers arriving after the launch email spike fades. Flipping them live on opening day, not the day after, means you catch the \"is it actually open\" curiosity and the warm audience while attention is at its peak. Every hour they sit in draft is paid attention you are not collecting."},{"h":"How","b":"1. In Meta Ads Manager, open the Winchester launch campaign. Switch the campaign, ad set and ad toggles all from Off to On: all three levels must be green or nothing delivers. 2. Confirm the ad set targets the agreed Winchester radius and age range, the daily budget is set, and the schedule starts today. 3. Read every line of ad copy once more and remove any prescription or POM treatment name before it goes live, and confirm the destination is the live booking page. 4. In Google Ads, open the Winchester campaign, set status to Enabled at campaign and ad group level, and confirm location targeting is Winchester, not the whole county. 5. Confirm both Google Business Profiles have finished reconnecting so the map and ads line up. 6. Twenty minutes after switching on, refresh Ads Manager to confirm status reads Active, not In review or Rejected, then check again this afternoon."}],"2026-11-02#915":[{"h":"Why","b":"A new clinic in a new town lives or dies on trust, and Google reviews are the trust strangers see before they ever meet you. Reviews left on opening day, when clients are delighted and it is a genuine occasion, are the fastest way to turn a bare new profile into one that actually converts the ad traffic you just switched on."},{"h":"How","b":"1. Print the Google review QR code, stand it on the reception desk, and save the direct review link in your phone notes. 2. Ask in person at the natural high point, usually right after the treatment when they are looking in the mirror. 3. If they say yes but are rushing, offer to text the link so they can do it in the car: \"Shall I pop the link over so it takes ten seconds?\" 4. Script to use out loud: \"This means the world on our opening day. If you have two minutes, a quick Google review really helps other Winchester women find us. I can text you the link right now if that is easier.\" 5. Never offer anything in exchange for a review, and never pressure. A warm ask on a happy day is plenty. 6. At close, note how many were left and who promised one, so you can send a gentle follow-up text tomorrow."}],"2026-11-03#921":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"Day two is about momentum and honest FOMO. Yesterday said \"we're open\"; today's stories say \"it is really happening and places are already going.\" Stories keep the launch top of feed for the warm audience without spending a second grid post, and they let you show the Founding cap ticking down, which is your strongest honest urgency."},{"h":"Creative","b":"A story series of 5 to 7 frames filmed through the day, not a grid post. Frame ideas: a real treatment-room moment (client with consent, or just your hands and product), a \"day two on Jewry Street\" selfie to camera, a close-up of the Founding Client card, a genuine count such as \"X of 40 places have gone,\" a quick answer to yesterday's most-asked question, and a final frame with the link sticker. Keep each frame under 15 seconds, natural light, and add a poll or question sticker on one frame to pull replies, which lifts reach."},{"h":"Copy","b":"On-screen hook for the opening frame: \"Day two, and Winchester, you've been lovely.\"\n\nText to type across the frames: \"Yesterday still hasn't sunk in. Thank you to everyone who came to Jewry Street. Honest update: Founding Client places are already going, and once 40 is gone it is gone for good. If you have been meaning to book, this is your nudge.\" End frame call to action: \"Tap the link to claim your Founding place.\"\n\nIf you save a frame to your grid, use this caption:\nDay two on Jewry Street and my heart is full. Founding places are going, capped at 40. Come and say hello, or tap the link.\n#WinchesterAesthetics #JewryStreet #NurseLedAesthetics #HampshireBeauty #WinchesterLife"}],"2026-11-03#922":[{"h":"Why","b":"The opening rush is over, so reviews now come from steady, ordinary days, and that steadiness is exactly what Google rewards. A trickle of fresh reviews week after week ranks your new profile far better than one big burst that then goes silent, and it keeps feeding your live ads with social proof."},{"h":"How","b":"1. Make the ask a fixed part of the appointment, not a one-off. Add a line to your end-of-treatment routine so it happens every single time without you having to remember. 2. Keep the QR code on the desk and the link pinned in your phone. 3. Vary the wording so it stays genuine, for example: \"If you are happy with how that felt, a quick Google review would honestly help us.\" 4. For anyone who promised one yesterday and has not left it, send one warm follow-up text with the direct link, then leave it. One nudge, never two. 5. Reply to every review within a day, by name, in your own voice: clients who see you engage are more likely to leave their own. 6. Set a simple target, say five new reviews a week for the first month, and glance at the count each Friday."}],"2026-11-04#931":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"After two days of \"we're open\" energy, this is the warm exhale that builds the human bond behind the brand. A highlights post reminds the warm audience why they follow you, a nurse who cares rather than a clinic that sells, and it earns the saves and shares that keep you in front of people still deciding. It also keeps the Founding cap gently visible without any hard sell."},{"h":"Creative","b":"A montage Reel or carousel of the best real moments from the first two days, stitched from the b-roll you have been capturing. Format: a 20 to 30 second Reel over soft, warm audio, or a 6 to 8 slide carousel. Include the sign, the treatment room, a candid of you mid-conversation with a client (consent), a happy client moment (consent), and one honest behind-the-scenes frame such as tidying up at the end of the day. On-screen hook on the first frame: \"Two days in. Here's what opening a clinic actually feels like.\" Keep it real, no polished stock feel."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Two days ago I unlocked a door on Jewry Street for the first time as its owner, and I have not stopped smiling since.\n\nI trained as a nurse because I wanted to look after people properly. This clinic is that, just in my own space, in a town I love. Every face through the door this week has reminded me why the honest, unhurried way is the only way I want to do this.\n\nThank you, Winchester. If you have been thinking about your skin, the Founding Client places are still going: priority booking, a complimentary skincare add-on with your first treatment, and founding pricing locked in for good, capped at 40.\n\nCome and say hello, or tap the link when you are ready.\n\n#WinchesterAesthetics #NurseLedAesthetics #JewryStreet #HampshireSkinClinic #WomenInBusiness #WinchesterHighStreet"}],"2026-11-05#941":[{"h":"What to check","b":"In Meta Ads Manager and Google Ads, pull the first three days of the live Winchester campaigns. Look at spend, link clicks, cost per link click, landing page views, and above all cost per booked analysis or consultation, not just cost per lead. Cross-check bookings in GHL against ad clicks so you know how many clicks actually became appointments, and note which creative and which platform is carrying the results."},{"h":"What good looks like","b":"Your pre-launch lead cost was about 3.08 pounds, so on cold launch traffic a cost per landing page view in the low single pounds is healthy. The number that really matters is cost per booked analysis: under roughly 25 to 35 pounds is strong for a premium nurse-led service where one client is worth hundreds over time. If clicks are cheap but bookings are not following, the bottleneck is the booking page, not the ad, so check it loads fast and the Founding offer is obvious. Pause any single ad that has spent more than about 15 pounds with zero bookings and shift that budget to the best performer."},{"h":"Retarget","b":"1. In Meta Ads Manager, Audiences, create a Custom Audience from your Facebook and Instagram engagers plus website visitors who reached the booking page but did not complete, over the last 7 days. 2. Build one small retargeting ad set at about 5 to 8 pounds a day aimed only at that audience. 3. Use a gentle reminder creative, not the launch ad: a short piece to camera from Abi saying the Founding places are limited and going, with the book link. 4. Exclude anyone who already booked so you are not paying to reach existing clients, then review again in three days."}],"2026-11-06#951":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"The launch buzz has introduced you; now the warm-but-undecided need proof that real people trust you. Genuine social proof from your first clients is the bridge between \"nice opening\" and \"I should book,\" and it directly warms the retargeting audience David just built. This is the post that converts the fence-sitters before the Founding cap fills."},{"h":"Creative","b":"A carousel or Reel featuring your first clients, used only with written consent on file. The safest and most powerful format is short talking-head clips of clients saying in their own words why they came and how it felt, or a quote card with their words and first name if they would rather not be filmed. If you show any skin, use only images you have specific written consent to post, and never imply a result you cannot honestly stand behind. On-screen hook: \"Winchester's first clients, in their own words.\" Keep captions on the video for silent viewers."},{"h":"Copy","b":"I could tell you why the honest, nurse-led way works. It means far more coming from the people who have actually sat in the chair.\n\nThese are some of Winchester's very first clients, sharing why they came and how it felt, in their own words and shared with their permission. No scripts, no pressure, just real people and real skin.\n\nThis is exactly what I hoped this clinic would feel like from day one.\n\nIf you have been waiting for a sign, this is it. Founding Client places are still open: priority booking, a complimentary skincare add-on with your first treatment, and founding pricing locked in for good, capped at 40. Tap the link to claim yours.\n\n#WinchesterAesthetics #NurseLedAesthetics #JewryStreet #HampshireSkinClinic #ClientLove #WinchesterHighStreet"}],"2026-11-08#1001":[{"h":"Purpose of this check-in","b":"This is your weekly Founding Client review, one week after opening. The whole November strategy hinges on filling those first 40 founding spots, so this is where you protect that goal. You are checking three things: how many of the 40 founding places are gone, whether the diary for the next two weeks has enough space for new bookings, and whether any warm Winchester leads have gone quiet and need a nudge. Twenty minutes with a coffee, same time every Sunday."},{"h":"What to review, in order","b":"1) Founding Client tally: open GHL, filter contacts by the 'Founding Client' tag and count them against 40. Write the live number somewhere visible (a whiteboard in clinic works). 2) Diary health: open the ANS booking calendar and eyeball the next 14 days. If any weekday has zero availability, you may be under-serving demand; if most slots are empty, we lean harder on the retarget ad (16 Nov) and the 'we're open' email (9 Nov). 3) Lead follow-up: in GHL, filter the 443 Winchester leads for anyone tagged as contacted but not booked, and flag the top 15 for a personal WhatsApp or SMS this week."},{"h":"Decisions to make and log","b":"Based on the founding tally, decide the messaging tone for the week: if you are past 25 of 40, the scarcity angle ('only a handful of founding places left') becomes true and powerful, so tell David to lead with it in the 9 Nov posts and email. If you are under 15, we hold the scarcity line and instead push the free-value angle harder. Note the number and the decision in a pinned message so David writes the week's copy to match. Also confirm Abi's filming slot for the week (see the batch-record needs for the 9, 11 and 13 Nov posts) is in the diary before Monday."}],"2026-11-09#1011":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"This is the Authority post, the backbone of the launch week. New Winchester audiences do not know Abi yet, so before we ask for a booking we establish the single most important trust signal: she is a registered nurse, not a beauty therapist with a needle. This post sits at the top of the funnel, warms the 443 leads who are now seeing organic content, and gives every later 'book now' post something to stand on. It earns the right to sell later in the month."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Format: Reel, 20 to 30 seconds, filmed inside the new 9A Jewry Street clinic so Winchester locals recognise the space. On-screen hook text for the first 2 seconds: 'A nurse opened a skin clinic on Jewry Street.' Abi talks straight to camera, calm and unhurried, at eye level in natural window light. B-roll to cut in: her NMC pin badge or lanyard, hands washing, the clean treatment room, the Winchester street view from the window. End on the clinic sign or door. No fast cuts, the calm pace IS the brand."},{"h":"Copy","b":"There is a difference between someone who took a weekend course and a nurse who trained for years to put a needle near your face. I am Abi, a registered nurse, and my new clinic is now open on Jewry Street in the heart of Winchester. Nurse-led means I read your skin and your health first, I say no when no is the honest answer, and I plan for your face in five years, not just this week. If you have wanted expert, natural skin care in Winchester, come and meet me. Tap the link to book, or comment WINCHESTER and I will send you the details. #WinchesterAesthetics #NurseLedSkincare #JewryStreet #HampshireAesthetics #WinchesterHighStreet"}],"2026-11-09#1012":[{"h":"What","b":"Re-engage every Winchester lead who has seen us open but has not yet booked. This email goes to the segment of the 443 warm leads who are not tagged as booked. The goal is one action: book a first treatment and claim a Founding Client place. We lead with the fact that the doors are actually open now (the wait is over) and we use gentle, honest scarcity: the founding bundle is capped at 40. No discount is mentioned because Winchester has none; the value is the locked-in founding pricing and the complimentary add-on."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Subject: The Jewry Street doors are open, {name}\n\nHi {name},\n\nIt is official. My new clinic at 9A Jewry Street in Winchester is open, and it was lovely to finally welcome the first clients through the door this week.\n\nYou put your name down a little while ago, so I wanted to tell you personally rather than let you find out from an ad.\n\nHere is the one thing worth knowing today. The first 40 people to start their skin journey with me become Founding Clients. That means priority booking whenever the diary is busy, a complimentary skincare add-on with your first treatment, and your founding pricing locked in for good, even as the clinic grows. It is capped at 40 on purpose, and places are going.\n\nThere is no rush to decide on a treatment. Your first step can simply be a proper conversation about your skin.\n\nBook your first visit here: {link}\n\nWarmly,\nAbi\nRegistered Nurse, Abi Peters Skin Clinic"}],"2026-11-11#1031":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"The Human post. People book a face they trust with a person they like, and week one is all first impressions. This post lowers the wall: it shows the woman behind the nurse title, why she chose Winchester, and what walking in actually feels like. It deepens the relationship with the leads warmed by Sunday's Authority Reel and makes the eventual booking feel like visiting someone, not buying a service. It also builds local pride, which is rocket fuel for a high street clinic."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Format: Reel, 25 to 30 seconds, softer and more personal than the Authority post. Hook text: 'Why I chose Winchester.' Film Abi walking up to or standing outside the Jewry Street door with the street behind her, then a warmer to-camera moment inside. B-roll: a favourite local coffee spot, the view down the street, her setting up the room in the morning, a candle or a small welcome touch. Warm, handheld, a little imperfect on purpose. Let her smile and be human, not polished."},{"h":"Copy","b":"I could have opened anywhere. I chose Winchester because it is the kind of place people actually care about doing things properly. I wanted my second clinic somewhere with a bit of heart, a proper high street, and clients who want honest advice over a hard sell. Jewry Street felt right the moment I saw it. When you come in, you will not get a conveyor belt. You will get a cup of something warm, a real look at your skin, and a nurse who tells you the truth. That is the whole idea. Come and say hello, the kettle is on. Tap the link to book your first visit. #Winchester #WinchesterHighStreet #NurseLedAesthetics #HampshireSkinClinic #JewryStreet"}],"2026-11-12#1041":[{"h":"Why","b":"Getting a client through the door once is expensive; getting them back costs almost nothing, and a happy client who refers a friend is the cheapest lead you will ever get. Right now those two things only happen if you remember to chase them by hand, which does not scale when the diary fills. Turning on automated rebooking and referral in GHL means every first-time Winchester client is gently invited back at the right moment, and asked to refer a friend when they are happiest, without you lifting a finger. This is what turns a launch spike into a steady book."},{"h":"How","b":"1) In GHL, go to Automation, then Workflows, then Create Workflow, and start from Recipes if a rebooking recipe is offered. 2) Rebooking workflow: trigger on 'Appointment Status = Completed'. Add a Wait step of about 3 to 4 weeks (match it to the treatment interval Abi advises). Then add an SMS and an email inviting them to book their next visit with a {link} to the ANS calendar. Save and set to Publish, not Draft. 3) Referral workflow: create a second workflow, trigger on 'Appointment Status = Completed', Wait 3 days (so they have seen results), then send one SMS thanking them and offering a simple referral ('send a friend my way and I will look after you both'). 4) Test both: in the workflow, use Test Workflow with your own contact record and confirm the SMS and email actually arrive with a working link. 5) Check the phone number and email sender are the clinic's, then flip both workflows to Publish. Note in the Founding check-in that they are live."}],"2026-11-13#1051":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"The Proof post. We have spent the week on trust and warmth; now we show that real people are already choosing the clinic. Social proof is the bridge between 'I like her' and 'I booked'. Even in week one you have proof: first clients through the door, early kind words, a fully booked launch day. This post reassures the fence-sitters among your 443 leads that they are not the first guinea pig, and it makes the founding cap feel real, places are genuinely being taken."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Format: carousel of 3 to 4 slides, or a static if time is short. Slide 1 hook: 'Week one in Winchester.' Slide 2: a real photo of the clinic mid-day, a client's hands, a thank-you card or flowers, anything true and un-staged (no faces without consent). Slide 3: a short genuine quote from an early client if you have written permission to share it, otherwise a line about how the first days felt. Slide 4: the ask, clean text on brand background, 'Founding Client places are going, book yours.' Keep it honest, do not invent reviews."},{"h":"Copy","b":"One week in, and I am a little emotional if I am honest. The first clients have been through the door on Jewry Street, and the kindness has been something else. This is the bit the training does not prepare you for: people trusting you with their skin, their confidence, sometimes their nerves. I do not take it lightly. If you have been waiting to see whether the new Winchester clinic is for you, it is, and there are still a handful of the first 40 Founding Client places left. That means your pricing locked in for good and a little something extra on your first visit. Tap the link when you are ready. #WinchesterAesthetics #NurseLed #HampshireBusiness #WinchesterHighStreet #SkinHealth"}],"2026-11-16#1111":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"The treatment spotlight. Until now the content has sold the nurse and the place; this post sells the 'what', for people who like Abi but are not sure what she can actually do for their specific concern. We pick one hero treatment that is safe to name (a skin booster, a facial, microneedling, never a POM), and we explain who it is for and why in plain English. This gives warm leads a concrete reason to book now and feeds the retarget ad going live the same day, so the paid and organic reinforce each other."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Format: Reel, 30 seconds, educational and calm. Hook text: 'If your skin looks tired but you do not know why.' Abi to camera explaining one concern (dullness, dehydration, early fine lines) and the one treatment that helps, in her honest-friend voice. B-roll: product or serum close-ups, the treatment room, gloved hands preparing, soft skin-texture shots. Absolutely no guaranteed-results language and no before-and-afters without written consent. Teach first, sell second."},{"h":"Copy","b":"If your skin looks a bit tired and flat lately and you cannot put your finger on why, it is very often not about wrinkles at all. It is hydration and glow, deep down, the stuff a moisturiser cannot reach. A skin booster works underneath the surface to bring back that fresh, well-rested look, the 'have you been on holiday' kind of skin. It is one of my favourite things to do because it looks like you, just better rested. Not sure if it is right for you? That is exactly what your first consultation is for. Tap the link and let us have a proper look together. #WinchesterAesthetics #SkinBoosters #GlowingSkin #NurseLedSkincare #HampshireAesthetics"}],"2026-11-16#1112":[{"h":"Objective","b":"Conversions, specifically booked consultations. This is a retargeting campaign, so the objective in Meta Ads Manager is Sales (or Leads if you optimise for the booking form), warm audience only. These people have already visited the website or engaged, so the job is not to introduce the clinic, it is to remove the last bit of friction and get them onto the ANS calendar. Optimise for the booking event, not link clicks."},{"h":"Audience","b":"Build a Custom Audience of everyone who visited the website in the last 30 days (from the pixel) plus everyone who engaged with the Instagram or Facebook page in the last 30 days, and layer in the 443 Winchester lead list as a Custom Audience too. Exclude anyone already tagged as booked so you are not paying to reach existing clients. Geography as a safety net: within 15 miles of Winchester, women, ages 30 to 55. Keep it tight; retargeting works because the audience is small and warm."},{"h":"Budget","b":"Start at 8 to 10 pounds per day for a warm retarget pool this size. It does not need much because the audience is small; spending more just burns frequency. Run it for 7 to 10 days, then judge on cost per booked consultation, not cost per click. If it is delivering bookings under about 15 to 20 pounds each, consider nudging to 12 pounds a day."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Use the strongest-performing organic Reel from the launch week (likely the Authority or Proof post) as the ad, so it already has real engagement and looks native. Add a clear single-frame end card or headline reinforcing the founding cap. One video, one image fallback. Keep the same calm nurse-led tone; a hard-sell creative to a warm audience feels jarring and kills trust."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Primary text: You had a look at the new Winchester clinic. Here is your nudge. I am Abi, a registered nurse, now open on Jewry Street, and the first 40 clients become Founding Clients with priority booking, a complimentary add-on on their first visit, and founding pricing locked in for good. Places are going. Your first step is simply a proper conversation about your skin, no pressure and no hard sell. Tap below to find a time that suits you.\nHeadline: Book your first visit in Winchester"}],"2026-11-18#1131":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"A second Human post, deliberately different from 11 Nov. The first humanised the founder and the choice of Winchester; this one humanises the standard of care, the honesty that defines nurse-led practice. The single most powerful, most repeatable thing Abi says is 'I will tell you if you do not need it.' A clinic that turns treatments away is a clinic you can trust with a yes. This post builds the deep trust that makes the higher-value bookings and memberships (23 Nov) land later, and it quietly separates Abi from the hard-sell salons nearby."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Format: Reel, 20 to 25 seconds, intimate and honest. Hook text: 'The treatment I talk people out of most.' Abi to camera, close and warm, telling a true (anonymised) story of a client who came in wanting something she advised against, and what she suggested instead. B-roll: a genuine consultation moment, her looking at skin under the clinic light, a reassuring hand. The emotional beat is relief, the feeling of finally being told the truth. No client faces without written consent."},{"h":"Copy","b":"The thing people are most surprised by is how often I say no. Someone will come in convinced they need a big treatment because an ad told them so, and I will look at their skin and say, honestly, you do not need that, here is what will actually help. It sometimes costs me a sale in the moment. I do not mind even slightly. I am a nurse first, and I would rather you trust me for the next ten years than sell you something today you did not need. That is what nurse-led really means. If you want that kind of honesty about your skin, my door on Jewry Street is open. Tap the link to book. #NurseLedAesthetics #HonestSkincare #WinchesterAesthetics #SkinHealth #HampshireClinic"}],"2026-11-20#1151":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"The results and Proof post, the one that shows the work, done with total ASA and consent discipline. By day 18 you may have a genuine early result from a first-week client, or at minimum you have honest testimonial words. This post is the closest we come to 'here is what you get', so it does the heaviest converting of the fortnight for people who need to see evidence before they book. It also anchors the reviews push happening the same day: a public result invites private enquiries and public reviews."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Format: carousel or Reel. If, and only if, you hold written consent, a real before-and-after may be shown; frame it honestly, same lighting, no filters, and describe the treatment and realistic timeline. If you do not have consent, do NOT stage or imply results, use a client's written quote on a clean brand slide instead, or Abi to camera reading a kind message aloud with permission. Hook text: 'Real skin, real result, real consent.' Never use guaranteed-results wording; say 'this is one person's result and everyone is different.'"},{"h":"Copy","b":"I will only ever show you real skin, and only ever with permission. This is a lovely client who kindly said yes to sharing her result after a course with me. Look at the difference in how rested and healthy the skin looks. I want to be clear and honest with you: this is one person's journey, everyone's skin is different, and I would never promise you an identical outcome. What I can promise is a proper assessment and a plan built around your skin, not a template. If you would like your own honest starting point, tap the link and book your first visit in Winchester. #WinchesterAesthetics #RealResults #NurseLedSkincare #SkinJourney #HampshireAesthetics"}],"2026-11-20#1152":[{"h":"Purpose of the reviews push","b":"Google reviews are the single biggest lever for a new clinic's local search and for winning strangers who search 'skin clinic Winchester'. Both Google Business Profiles were expired and are being reconnected, which means you are starting close to zero on the new listing, so early reviews matter enormously. The target is a clear, realistic 10 Google reviews this month. This is a founding-team job (both owners), because it needs Abi's personal ask in clinic and David's systems behind it. Do this on the same day as the results post so the momentum feeds itself."},{"h":"How to hit 10 this month","b":"1) Confirm the Winchester Google Business Profile is reconnected and live, and grab the short review link (from the profile, use 'Get more reviews' to copy the direct link). 2) In GHL, build or switch on a review-request workflow triggered on 'Appointment Completed', Wait 2 days, then send one SMS: 'Hi {name}, it was lovely to see you at the clinic. If you have two minutes, a Google review genuinely helps a new local business like mine so much: {link}. No pressure at all, Abi.' 3) The human layer, this is what actually works: Abi asks warmly in person at the end of a good appointment, 'if you have a spare minute later, a Google review would mean the world', then the automated SMS lands as the reminder. 4) Make it frictionless: a small QR code to the review link on the reception desk. 5) Never incentivise reviews (against Google's rules) and never write them yourself. Track the count weekly at the Sunday check-in against the 10 goal."}],"2026-11-23#1211":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"The memberships and packages post. So far every post has sold a single visit; this one introduces the higher-value, higher-loyalty offer for people who are now warmed up and ready to commit to their skin properly. Skin results come from consistency, not one-offs, which is the honest clinical truth and the perfect reason for a package or membership. This post lifts average client value, smooths your diary into predictable repeat bookings, and rewards the people who already trust Abi. It naturally follows the results post two days earlier: 'you saw the result, here is how you actually get there.'"},{"h":"Creative","b":"Format: carousel, clean and considered, or a calm Reel. Hook text: 'One treatment is a nice day. A plan is a change.' Slide or segment structure: 1) the honest truth that skin responds to consistency, 2) what a package or membership includes in plain terms, 3) who it suits (someone ready to invest in their skin over months, not chase quick fixes), 4) the founding link, noting founding pricing is locked in. Abi voiceover keeps it educational, never pushy. Show the room, the products, the calm ritual of a regular visit."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Here is the honest truth no one selling you a single treatment wants to say out loud: your skin changes with consistency, not with one lovely afternoon. That is why I build plans, not one-offs. A skin plan with me means we map where you want to get to, we space your treatments the way your skin actually needs, and you stop guessing and start seeing steady, natural progress. For my Founding Clients that pricing is locked in for good, which makes committing to your skin properly a lot easier on the purse. If you are ready to stop dabbling and actually get somewhere, let us build your plan together. Tap the link or send me a message. #WinchesterAesthetics #SkinPlan #NurseLedSkincare #SkinHealth #HampshireAesthetics"}],"2026-11-24#1221":[{"h":"What","b":"Warm up the whole list for Black Friday and Christmas gifting before the offer actually drops. This is a teaser, not the sale, its only job is to build anticipation and stop people spending their gifting budget elsewhere this week. It goes to the full warm list (Winchester leads and any Bedhampton clients on the CRM). We create a small sense of 'wait for mine', position gift vouchers as the thoughtful, non-tat present, and tell them exactly when to look out for the real email. No prescription treatments named, gifting is framed around vouchers and skincare, which sidesteps any POM issue entirely."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Subject: Before you buy the candle, {name}, wait for this\n\nHi {name},\n\nQuick one, because I do not want you to miss it.\n\nThis Friday I am opening a small Black Friday and Christmas gifting window, and I wanted you on the list to hear first before anyone else.\n\nIf there is someone in your life who always says they would love to look after their skin but never quite treats themselves, a gift from the clinic is a lovely, genuinely used present. Far better than another candle that sits in a drawer. Think vouchers they can spend on the treatment or skincare that is right for them, chosen with a nurse, not guessed at.\n\nI will send you the full details and how to buy on Friday. For now, just keep a little of your gifting budget back for me.\n\nSee you Friday,\nAbi\nRegistered Nurse, Abi Peters Skin Clinic\n\nP.S. If it is your own skin you have been meaning to sort, the founding places at the Winchester clinic are still open too: {link}"}],"2026-11-25#1231":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"The last Human post of the month, timed to land as the gifting and Black Friday push begins, so it keeps the feed warm and trustworthy right when the content turns more commercial. A human beat here stops the run of sell-y posts from feeling like a sale. The theme is gratitude and reflection: nearly a month in Winchester, what it has meant. This deepens loyalty with everyone who has already booked, and makes the gifting message that follows feel like it comes from someone the audience genuinely likes, not a brand cashing in on Christmas."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Format: Reel, 25 to 30 seconds, warm and reflective. Hook text: 'Nearly a month on Jewry Street.' Abi to camera at the end of a working day, softer light, a little tired and happy. She reflects on the first month, names a real moment that stuck with her (a nervous client who left smiling, a kind review, a local who popped in to say welcome). B-roll: the clinic at dusk, lights on, the Winchester street quiet, tidying up, a candle. Let it breathe. This one is about feeling, not booking."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Nearly a month on Jewry Street, and I keep having little moments where I have to stop and take it in. Someone came in this week genuinely nervous, sat down, and left half an hour later standing that bit taller. That is the whole reason I do this. Not the treatments themselves, but what they do for how someone feels walking back out onto the high street. Thank you, Winchester, for the warmest welcome I could have hoped for. If we have not met yet, I would love to change that. My door is open, and the kettle, as ever, is on. #Winchester #NurseLedAesthetics #WinchesterHighStreet #HampshireBusiness #Gratitude"}],"2026-11-27#1251":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"The Christmas gifting kick-off, the post that turns Friday's teaser email into a public, shoppable moment. This is peak gifting-decision season, and the strategy is to own the 'thoughtful present' position before people default to the usual high-street gifts. Vouchers are the hero because they are POM-safe, flexible, and margin-friendly, and they bring brand-new people (the gift recipients) into the clinic in January, seeding next quarter. This post works the top and bottom of the funnel at once: gift-buyers act now, and their recipients become new leads."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Format: carousel, festive but tasteful, staying on brand (no tacky Christmas clip-art). Hook text on slide 1: 'The gift she will actually use.' Slides: 1) the hook, 2) what a gift voucher is and how thoughtful it is, chosen-with-a-nurse framing, 3) how to buy (in clinic or via {link}), 4) a gentle deadline nudge for Christmas. Optional Reel alternative: Abi wrapping or handing over a gift voucher card, warm and simple. Show a beautifully presented physical voucher if you have one, the object makes it feel giftable. Calm, warm, premium."},{"h":"Copy","b":"The candle is lovely. The bath set is lovely. She will also never use them and you both know it. Here is the gift that actually gets opened again and again: time on her own skin, with a nurse who genuinely listens. My Christmas gift vouchers are here, and they are the loveliest thing to give the person who spends all year looking after everyone else. She chooses when to come in, and we choose together what her skin actually needs, no guessing. Pop in to Jewry Street to pick one up, or tap the link to buy in two minutes from your sofa. Give something she will remember long after the tree comes down. #ChristmasGifts #WinchesterAesthetics #GiftHerGlow #NurseLedSkincare #HampshireChristmas"}],"2026-11-30#1311":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"This is the gentle door-opener for the whole December gifting run, posted the day before the voucher campaign goes live so the idea lands before any hard sell. It reframes 'what do I get her' into 'give her skin she feels good in', the emotional hook that carries every gift post this month. Aim for saves and shares rather than clicks: top-of-funnel warmth that primes the 443 Winchester leads and both local audiences for tomorrow. With both clinics now open, it quietly signals that Winchester high street gifting is a real option this year."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Film a 15 to 20 second Reel: a slow, cosy unboxing of a wrapped skincare set and a gift card on a clean surface, warm December light, a candle just in shot. On-screen hook text for the first three seconds: 'The gift she won't quietly return.' Add two or three b-roll cutaways: hands tying ribbon, the Jewry Street window, a serum bottle catching the light. Keep it tactile with no voiceover, soft instrumental audio."},{"h":"Copy","b":"The one gift she won't quietly return in January. Every year someone unwraps another candle they didn't need. This year, give her the thing she actually wants but never books for herself: expert, nurse-led skin care, on her terms. A gift card she can spend on a treatment or the skincare that genuinely suits her, at Winchester or Bedhampton, whenever she is ready. No pressure, no expiry panic, just twenty minutes that are entirely hers. Save this for the person who is impossible to buy for. Vouchers go live tomorrow, comment GIFT and I will send you the details first. #WinchesterAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #NurseLedAesthetics #SkincareGifts #WinchesterHighStreet #ChristmasGiftIdeas"}],"2026-12-01#1321":[{"h":"What","b":"Formally open the Christmas campaign to the full list (both clinics plus the 443 Winchester leads), turning warm interest into voucher and skincare sales. The goal is a first wave of gift purchases early in December while budgets are fresh and diaries are open. One clear primary action: buy a gift voucher or a skincare gift via the link. Set the calm, generous tone that separates Abi from the pushy December noise."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Subject: The gift she keeps (and actually uses)\nPreview: Skin care vouchers and gift sets, ready when you are.\n\nHi {name},\n\nEvery December I get the same quiet question: 'What do I get someone who says they don't want anything?'\n\nHere is my honest answer. Give them something that feels like being looked after. Not another gadget, not another candle, but time with someone who actually knows skin.\n\nOur Christmas gift cards are ready. They can be spent on any treatment or on the skincare I would genuinely recommend, at either the Winchester or Bedhampton clinic, with no rush to book and plenty of time to redeem. If you would rather give something to unwrap, our curated skincare gift sets are here too, chosen by me, nothing on the list I would not use myself.\n\nYou choose the amount or the set, we do the rest, beautifully presented and ready to give.\n\nBrowse gift cards and sets: {link}\n\nAny questions, just reply to this email, it comes straight to us.\n\nWarmly,\nAbi"}],"2026-12-01#1322":[{"h":"Objective","b":"Run a Sales objective campaign (conversions) on Meta pointing to the gift voucher and skincare purchase page, with the pixel firing on Purchase or Initiate Checkout. On Google, run a Search campaign on gift and voucher intent plus a light Performance Max feed for the skincare products. The single measurable goal is voucher and skincare revenue, not reach, so optimise to the purchase event and let it run at least a week before judging."},{"h":"Audience","b":"Core Meta audience: women aged 30 to 55 within a 12 mile radius of both Winchester (SO23) and Havant or Bedhampton (PO9), interests around skincare, beauty and self-care gifting. Layer a warm retargeting ad set over the 443 existing leads, all Instagram and Facebook engagers from the last 365 days, and website visitors, these convert cheapest. Add a 1 percent lookalike of past enquirers for prospecting. On Google keep Search tight to local gift intent and exclude broad national terms."},{"h":"Budget","b":"Start at 15 pounds a day on Meta, split roughly 6 pounds prospecting and 9 pounds retargeting, since warm audiences will carry the early sales. Put 8 pounds a day on Google Search plus 5 pounds on Performance Max for skincare. Total around 28 pounds a day: review after 5 to 7 days and shift budget toward whichever ad set brings the lowest cost per purchase. Wind it down before Christmas week when redemption panic drops conversion."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Lead Meta creative is a 4 by 5 Reel of the wrapped gift card and skincare set from the 30 November shoot, warm and tactile, first frame reading 'The gift she won't return.' Add a static carousel: slide one the gift card, slide two a skincare set, slide three 'Winchester and Bedhampton', slide four 'Redeem any time in the new year.' Keep it strictly gifting and skincare, show no injectable or prescription treatment and name none. Use a clean shot of the Jewry Street window as the local proof frame."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Primary text: Stuck for the person who says they want nothing? Give them expert, nurse-led skin care they would never book for themselves. Our Christmas gift cards work on any treatment or the skincare we actually recommend, at our Winchester high street and Bedhampton clinics. Choose the amount, we present it beautifully, they redeem whenever they are ready in the new year. Order online in two minutes.\nHeadline: Skin care gift cards, ready to give\nGoogle Search headlines: 'Skincare Gift Vouchers Winchester', 'Nurse-Led Clinic Gift Card', 'Christmas Skin Gifts Hampshire'. Description: 'Gift cards and skincare sets from a nurse-led Winchester and Bedhampton clinic. Order online, redeem any time.'"}],"2026-12-02#1331":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"Convert the gifting idea into concrete choices so nobody stalls on 'which one'. A gift guide is highly saveable and shareable, and it does the deciding for a busy buyer, which shortens the path to purchase the day after launch. Position it across price points so it works for a colleague, a sister and a partner alike. This carousel becomes an evergreen pinned reference for the whole month."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Carousel, five slides. Slide 1 cover: 'Abi's Christmas skin gift guide, sorted.' Slides 2 to 4 each feature one option with a clean flat-lay photo: the gift card (any amount), a glow starter skincare duo, a full pamper larger set. Slide 5: how to order plus 'Winchester and Bedhampton.' Shoot flat-lays from directly above on a pale linen background, natural window light, consistent framing across every slide."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Your entire Christmas list, sorted in one scroll. I have done the hard part for you. Three gifts, three budgets, zero standing in a shop wondering if she already owns it. One: the gift card, any amount, spent on whatever her skin actually needs. Two: the glow starter duo, the two products I reach for most. Three: the full pamper set for the person who deserves proper looking after. All chosen by me, all redeemable at Winchester or Bedhampton whenever suits. Save this so you are not panic-buying on the 23rd. Tap the link to order, or comment GUIDE and I will send it over. #HampshireAesthetics #WinchesterAesthetics #SkincareGifts #ChristmasGiftGuide #NurseLedSkincare #WinchesterHighStreet"}],"2026-12-04#1351":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"A direct, unhurried nudge aimed at the people who saw the guide and thought 'maybe' but did not act. This post exists to drive the click and the first meaningful spike of voucher sales, leaning on the practical wins of a gift card over a physical present. Keep it low pressure to stay on brand while still being clearly a call to buy. Retarget engagers of the last two posts with this same message via the paid campaign."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Short talking-to-camera Reel, 15 to 25 seconds, Abi in the Winchester clinic. On-screen hook: 'The lazy person's perfect gift.' Abi holds up a gift card to camera, warm and slightly playful. B-roll: the card being slipped into an envelope, the clinic interior behind her. Phone at eye level, natural light from the window, soft background."},{"h":"Copy","b":"The perfect gift for people who leave it late (I see you). A gift card takes ninety seconds to buy, never needs a size or a guess, and does not sit unused in a drawer. She picks the treatment or the skincare, she books when life calms down, and you look like you put in real thought. Honestly, it is the gift I would want. Winchester or Bedhampton, any amount, sent the same day. Tap the link and cross one name off your list right now. #WinchesterAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #LastMinuteGift #SkincareGifts #NurseLedAesthetics #Havant"}],"2026-12-07#1411":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"Widen the gifting idea beyond the obvious 'for her' buyer with a fresh angle: self-gifting and the treat-yourself permission slip that December quietly gives people. This keeps the campaign from feeling repetitive and captures the woman buying for everyone else who deserves something too. It re-engages the audience mid-run with a warmer, more personal note before the last-order urgency begins. Funnel: mid, nudging warm followers toward a first purchase for themselves."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Reel or single reflective static. If Reel: a cosy first-person scene, a cup of tea, the skincare routine at the end of a long day, on-screen hook 'This one is for you, actually.' If static: a beautifully lit shot of a skincare set with a short caption overlay. Keep the mood calm, honest and unstaged, December evening light, slow movement."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Permission slip: you are allowed to buy the nice thing for yourself too. You have spent all month thinking about everyone else's list. Here is a quiet reminder that the woman doing the wrapping, the planning and the remembering deserves twenty minutes that are just hers. A skin analysis in the new year, the serum you keep not buying, a treatment you have been curious about for ages. Pop it in your own basket, wrap it if you like, no one is judging. Winchester and Bedhampton, redeem whenever January calms down. Tap the link, this one is for you. #NurseLedAesthetics #WinchesterAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #SelfCare #SkinHealth #TreatYourself"}],"2026-12-09#1431":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"A pure trust and connection post with no ask, dropped mid-campaign so the feed does not feel like one long sales pitch. 'Human' posts remind people there is a real nurse behind the brand, which is the entire reason they choose Abi over a high-street chain. December is emotional and busy, so a genuine warm moment earns saves, comments and the goodwill that makes the next sales post land. It also humanises the new Winchester clinic for leads who have not visited yet."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Talking-to-camera Reel, 20 to 30 seconds, Abi in the Winchester clinic near the end of a working day, lights on, a bit of festive decoration in shot. No script, just honest: what this first Christmas with the Jewry Street doors open actually feels like. On-screen hook: 'Our first Christmas on Winchester high street.' Let it be slightly imperfect, that is the point, natural light plus a warm lamp, phone at eye level."},{"h":"Copy","b":"I still can't quite believe I get to write this. Our first Christmas with the Winchester doors open. A year ago this was a plan on a kitchen table and a lot of nervous late nights. Now there are fairy lights in the window on Jewry Street, clients I already feel lucky to know, and a second clinic full of the work I love most. I trained as a nurse because I wanted to actually help people, and getting to do that here, in a city I love, is not lost on me for a second. Thank you for following along, for the bookings, the kind messages, all of it. Genuinely. Here is to a lovely, calm end to the year. #WinchesterAesthetics #NurseLedAesthetics #WinchesterHighStreet #HampshireBusiness #SmallBusiness #JewryStreet"}],"2026-12-10#1441":[{"h":"What","b":"Create gentle urgency with the physical last-order deadline while opening the second revenue line: January bookings. Two jobs in one send: catch anyone still deciding on a posted gift before the cut-off, and get keen clients to claim a January slot before the diary fills after the break. Primary action is the link, secondary is a diary date to remember. Keep it helpful and organised rather than panicky."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Subject: Last posting date: Friday 19 December\nPreview: Plus, January is filling faster than usual.\n\nHi {name},\n\nA quick, useful one so nothing catches you out.\n\nIf you would like a physical gift card or a skincare set posted in time for Christmas, please order by Friday 19 December. After that, our digital gift cards are still available right up to Christmas Eve, they land instantly in an inbox and look just as lovely, so you are never truly stuck.\n\nOrder gift cards and sets here: {link}\n\nAnd one thing worth knowing early. January is our busiest booking month, and with the Winchester clinic now open the diary is filling faster than usual. If you already know you want a fresh start for your skin in the new year, it is worth claiming your slot now rather than in the January rush. Just reply with 'January' and we will hold you a time.\n\nHere whenever you need us,\nAbi"}],"2026-12-11#1451":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"This is the urgency post that converts fence-sitters using a hard, specific deadline. Deadlines outperform every other gifting message because they remove 'I'll do it later', which is where most sales quietly die. It also positions the digital voucher as the pressure-free fallback so no one feels shut out after the cut-off. Push this to warm retargeting audiences too, as they are closest to buying."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Bold, simple static or 10 second Reel built around one date. On-screen text large and clear: 'Posted gifts: order by Fri 19 Dec.' Second frame or slide: 'Digital cards: available till Christmas Eve.' Use brand colours, clean festive styling, minimal words so it reads in a half-second scroll. If Reel, a quick hand-addressing-an-envelope clip under the text."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Mark the date so future you is not panicking. If you want a gift card or skincare set posted in time for the big day, order by Friday 19 December. Miss it? Do not worry, our digital gift cards are ready right up to Christmas Eve, they arrive instantly and look every bit as thoughtful. Either way, it is skin care from a nurse-led clinic on Winchester high street, redeemable whenever the new year suits them. Tap the link and get it sorted while you are thinking about it. #WinchesterAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #ChristmasGifts #LastOrderDates #SkincareGifts #NurseLed"}],"2026-12-14#1511":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"Own the last-minute market, which in December is enormous and under-served by clinics that stop posting after mid-month. This post targets the panicked buyer with the reassurance that a great gift is still one tap away, converting stress into a sale. It leans fully on the instant digital voucher as the hero. Timed just before the posting cut-off to catch both the organised and the running-late."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Quick, punchy Reel, 12 to 18 seconds, phone screen or hands showing how fast a digital voucher is bought and sent. On-screen hook: 'Forgot someone? Ninety seconds, sorted.' Show the three taps: choose amount, add a name, send. End frame: the e-voucher looking beautiful on a phone. Bright, upbeat, natural light."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Forgot someone? Deep breath, I have got you. A digital gift card takes about ninety seconds and arrives instantly, no post, no queue, no telltale panic. Choose the amount, add a message, done, and it looks genuinely lovely landing in their inbox. They redeem it against any treatment or skincare at Winchester or Bedhampton whenever the new year calms down. Nobody ever has to know you did it in your dressing gown at 10pm. Tap the link and be the hero. #LastMinuteGifts #WinchesterAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #SkincareGifts #DigitalGiftCard #NurseLedAesthetics"}],"2026-12-15#1521":[{"h":"What","b":"The final nudge before the posting deadline and the strongest January booking push while attention is still on the clinic. This send should feel like a friendly last call, not a fire alarm: catch the last posted-gift buyers and convert the 'I'll sort my skin in January' crowd into actual held slots. Two clear actions, ordered by urgency: order a gift now, or claim a January time. After this, email goes quiet until the 22nd."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Subject: Final call: posted gifts and your January slot\nPreview: Friday is the cut-off, and January is nearly full.\n\nHi {name},\n\nTwo quick things before the Christmas quiet sets in.\n\nFirst, this is your last reminder that Friday 19 December is the cut-off for posted gift cards and skincare sets. If you would still like something to unwrap, order by then. After Friday, our digital cards are ready right up to Christmas Eve.\n\nOrder here: {link}\n\nSecond, and honestly the more important one: January slots are going. It is always our busiest month, and this year, with Winchester open alongside Bedhampton, the early January diary is filling faster than we expected. If you have been meaning to book a skin analysis or a treatment to start the year properly, now is the moment to lock in a time you actually want rather than whatever is left.\n\nReply 'January' and we will hold your slot, no payment needed today.\n\nThat is me done bothering your inbox until after Christmas. Thank you, truly, for a wonderful year.\n\nWarmly,\nAbi"}],"2026-12-16#1531":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"A warm, no-ask gratitude post that deepens loyalty right as the selling season peaks, so the audience remembers the feeling, not just the offers. Thank-you content consistently earns the highest saves and comments because it invites people to reflect and reply. It also seeds word of mouth: grateful founders get tagged and recommended. This softens the run of sales posts and keeps the brand feeling human going into the holidays."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Simple, heartfelt Reel or a carousel of real moments from the year: the Winchester fit-out, opening day, a few clinic details, the team, festive touches. On-screen hook: 'What a year to be grateful for.' If a talking-to-camera Reel, keep it short and genuine, 20 to 25 seconds, Abi thanking clients directly. Warm tones, real photos over polish."},{"h":"Copy","b":"I keep coming back to one word this month: grateful. This time last year, the Winchester clinic was a set of keys and a very long to-do list. Since then we have opened the doors on Jewry Street, welcomed our founding clients, and I have got to do the work I love in two places I am proud of. None of that happens without you: the people who booked, who trusted a nurse-led approach over the quick fix, who told a friend. Thank you. Whatever your December looks like, I hope it holds a bit of calm and a lot of the good stuff. See you in the new year. #WinchesterAesthetics #NurseLedAesthetics #ThankYou #HampshireAesthetics #WinchesterHighStreet #SmallBusiness"}],"2026-12-18#1551":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"Blend festive warmth with credibility so the final gifting push carries genuine proof behind it. A real client's words are the most persuasive asset available and answer the quiet 'is this actually good' question for anyone still hesitating on a voucher. Framed festively, it feels like a warm recommendation rather than a hard sell. It reinforces the nurse-led difference at the exact moment buyers are comparing options."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Carousel or static featuring one genuine written review (text only, with the client's written permission) on a tasteful festive-brand background, plus a clean clinic photo. No before and after images. On-screen hook: 'The nicest thing a client said this year.' Keep typography clean and readable, brand colours with a subtle seasonal touch. If a Reel, let the quote animate on gently over calm b-roll of the clinic."},{"h":"Copy","b":"The nicest thing a client said to me this year, and why I do this. She told me she finally understood her own skin for the first time, and that no one had ever actually explained it to her before. That is the whole job, honestly. Not selling the trendy thing, but sitting down, looking properly, and telling you the truth about what your skin needs. It is also exactly what a gift card gives someone: not a product guess, but expert time that is theirs. If that is the kind of care you want to give this Christmas, the link is right there. Winchester and Bedhampton, redeem in the new year. #NurseLedAesthetics #WinchesterAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #ClientLove #SkinHealth #SkincareGifts"}],"2026-12-21#1611":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"A genuine seasonal sign-off that closes the gifting chapter with grace and sets up the January pivot without selling. It communicates the practical (festive closing and reopening dates) while leaving the audience with warmth, which is exactly what people want from a brand on the 21st. Low effort to make, high goodwill return, and it keeps the page active over the quiet days. It plants the 'new year, fresh start' seed the January content will harvest."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Cosy static or short Reel: the clinic looking festive, lights low, a 'back in January' note styled on brand. On-screen text: 'Wishing you a lovely Christmas from Winchester and Bedhampton.' Include the closing and reopening dates clearly on the final frame. Warm, still, unhurried, candlelight or fairy-light glow."},{"h":"Copy","b":"From both clinics, with a full heart: happy Christmas. Thank you for making our first Winchester Christmas one I will never forget. We are closing from the 24th and reopening on the 2nd of January, refreshed and ready for whatever your skin goals are next year. However you are spending the next few days, I hope they are calm, cosy and kind to you. Look after yourself, drink the water, wear the SPF even in December (yes, really), and I will see you in the new year. If you fancy starting January with a proper plan for your skin, keep an eye here, I have something lovely coming. #WinchesterAesthetics #HappyChristmas #NurseLedAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #WinchesterHighStreet #Bedhampton"}],"2026-12-22#1621":[{"h":"What","b":"Pivot the full list from gifting to self-investment with the January 'new year, new skin' pre-sell, capturing intent while people are naturally goal-setting. The goal is to fill the January diary before the rush and to reframe resolutions away from crash fixes toward a calm, expert skin plan. One clear action: book or register interest in a January skin analysis via the link. Sent while inboxes are quiet and reflective, which suits a considered message."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Subject: New year, new skin (the calm, honest version)\nPreview: Start January with a plan, not another crash fix.\n\nHi {name},\n\nSomewhere between the leftover cheese and the new-year resolutions, a lot of us decide 'right, this is the year I sort my skin out.'\n\nI love that energy. I just want to point it somewhere that actually works.\n\nNot a crash routine you abandon by February. Not ten new products bought in a panic. Just a proper skin analysis: twenty minutes where I look at your skin under good light and tell you, honestly, what is going on and what it actually needs. Then a plan that fits your life, your budget and your skin, not a trend.\n\nThat is how we are starting the year at Winchester and Bedhampton, and I would love to see you there.\n\nThe January diary is already filling, so if you want first pick of times, register your interest now and we will get you booked in before it opens fully.\n\nStart your year here: {link}\n\nWishing you a wonderful new year,\nAbi"}],"2026-12-23#1631":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"A short teaser that builds anticipation for the January offer without revealing everything, keeping the audience curious over the dead week between Christmas and new year. Teasers work because they open a loop the brain wants closed, earning follows, saves and 'tell me more' comments. It bridges the festive close and the January launch so momentum does not go cold. It sets the expectation that January's approach will be calm and expert, not another gimmick."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Very short Reel, 8 to 15 seconds, intriguing and clean. On-screen hook: 'January is going to be different.' Quick, calm glimpses: the clinic, a diary opening, a serum, a hand writing 'skin reset' on a note. End on a 'coming soon' style frame. No full detail, just mood and a promise. Soft, hopeful music, natural light."},{"h":"Copy","b":"January is going to be different, and I am quietly excited. No crash fixes. No panic-buying seven products you will forget by February. Just a calmer, smarter way to actually understand your skin and build a plan that lasts. I am opening something lovely for the new year at both clinics, and the diary is already filling. I will share the full details in a few days, but if you want to be first in the queue, drop a 'ME' in the comments and I will make sure you hear before anyone else. Winchester and Bedhampton. Watch this space. #WinchesterAesthetics #NewYearNewSkin #NurseLedAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #SkinReset #ComingSoon"}],"2026-12-28#1711":[{"h":"Strategy","b":"Launch the January offer to the primed audience, converting all the teased anticipation and resolution energy into booked slots. This is the payoff post for the December run and the top of the January funnel, so it must be crystal clear on what the offer is and how to claim it. The offer is a complimentary New Year Skin Reset analysis plus a personalised plan, which mirrors the proven skin-analysis lead magnet and stays fully within the rules: no discount claims, no treatment promises. Urgency comes from limited January slots, not price cutting."},{"h":"Creative","b":"Confident talking-to-camera Reel, 20 to 30 seconds, Abi in the Winchester clinic, warm new-year energy. On-screen hook: 'Your January skin reset starts here.' Abi explains the offer simply and looks straight down the lens. B-roll: the analysis in action (clinic, good light, no client faces without consent), a diary, a personalised plan being written. Phone at eye level, clean and bright."},{"h":"Copy","b":"Here it is: your New Year Skin Reset, and it starts with the truth. Book a complimentary skin analysis at Winchester or Bedhampton this January and we will spend twenty focused minutes actually understanding your skin, then map out a simple, honest plan built around you. No pressure to book anything on the day, no crash-fix nonsense, just expert nurse-led guidance so you start the year knowing exactly what your skin needs. I only have a limited number of these slots in January and they are going quickly. Comment RESET or tap the link to claim yours. Let's make this the year your skin actually gets looked after. #NewYearNewSkin #WinchesterAesthetics #NurseLedAesthetics #HampshireAesthetics #SkinReset #SkinHealth"}],"2026-12-29#1721":[{"h":"What to check","b":"Pull full-year and Q4 numbers side by side for every channel: Meta Ads Manager (spend, leads, cost per lead, and crucially cost per booked analysis), Google Ads (spend, conversions, cost per booking), email (sends, bookings attributed) and organic social (reach, saves, DM and comment bookings). For each channel, calculate cost per booked consultation, not just cost per lead, by tracing GHL opportunities back to their source tag. Look hard at the 443 Winchester leads: how many have now booked or bought, and at what blended cost. List the three best performing posts and ad sets and the three worst by cost per booking."},{"h":"What good looks like","b":"The pre-launch benchmark was about 3.08 pounds per lead, but cost per booked analysis is the number that pays the bills: under roughly 25 to 35 pounds per booked consultation is healthy for a premium clinic, and warm retargeting should sit well below prospecting. Email should be your cheapest channel per booking by a distance, since the list is already warm, if it is not, the tracking or the offer is off. Keep and scale anything beating those numbers (usually retargeting and email), pause anything where cost per booking runs high with no redemption, and carry the top three creative angles into January. Write a one-line verdict per channel: scale, keep, fix or cut."}]};
